\begin{abstract}
In multi-waveguide pinching-antenna systems (PASS), waveguides are typically modeled as independent signal-transport paths. However, when dielectric waveguides are closely spaced, their evanescent electromagnetic fields overlap, allowing guided signals to exchange energy before radiation. While such inter-waveguide coupling is conventionally regarded as undesirable crosstalk, this paper reveals an opportunity to harness it as an additional analog beamforming resource. The key insight is that coupling, through its interaction with sequential signal extraction by distributed pinching antennas (PAs), can reshape coherent signal combining at the receivers without increasing the available signal power. Consequently, stronger coupling does not necessarily yield better performance; rather, the coupling-induced phase relationships determine whether the radiated signals combine constructively or destructively. To characterize and exploit this mechanism, we establish a physics-based end-to-end channel model, derive coupling-aware waveguide-spacing principles and achievable-rate bounds, and develop joint waveguide deployment, PA placement, and digital precoding algorithms for multi-hotspot communications. Numerical results demonstrate substantial sum-rate improvements over conventional fixed-deployment and optimized minimum-spacing PASS benchmarks.
These findings establish inter-waveguide evanescent coupling as a new physical degree of freedom for PASS channel reconfiguration.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Pinching antenna systems, coupled-waveguide pinching antennas, coupled-mode theory,  near-field modeling, 6G.
\end{IEEEkeywords}
\section{Introduction}
Pinching-antenna systems (PASS) have recently attracted considerable attention as a promising architecture for reconfigurable wireless communications \cite{ding2025flexible,xu2026generalized}. By transporting radio-frequency (RF) signals through low-loss dielectric waveguides and extracting them into free space using pinching antennas (PAs), PASS enables flexible radiation at locations close to intended users \cite{liu2026pinching}. Unlike conventional antenna arrays with fixed radiating elements, PASS can adapt its radiation geometry to changing user distributions, thereby reducing free-space propagation losses and improving communication performance \cite{suzuki2022pinching,wang2025modeling,zhang2026directional,wei2026multi}. Such flexibility is particularly appealing in multi-hotspot scenarios \cite{xu2026environmentaware,ding2026movable}, where communication demands are concentrated in localized regions. To further enhance spatial flexibility and multi-user transmission capabilities, recent studies have extended PASS from single-waveguide configurations to multi-waveguide architectures, where multiple RF-fed waveguides and their associated PAs can be jointly configured to serve distributed users \cite{11165763,11212813}.
A growing body of research has investigated how to exploit the spatial reconfigurability of PASS. Early studies focused on PA placement \cite{chen2025dynamic,xie2025low}, signal extraction \cite{wang2025modeling,11263923}, and beamforming design \cite{11371592,wang2025modeling}, demonstrating substantial performance benefits through flexible radiation positioning. Subsequent works extended these investigations to multi-waveguide systems, addressing antenna activation \cite{11165763}, hybrid beamforming \cite{chen2026hybrid,11223640,zhang2024polarization}, interference management \cite{11223640}, and achievable-rate characterization \cite{11358830,tyrovolas2025performance}. More recently, movable-waveguide PASS has been proposed to further adapt the waveguide arrangement itself to the spatial distribution of users \cite{ding2026movable}. Despite these advances, existing multi-waveguide PASS designs predominantly rely on an important simplifying assumption: different waveguides are treated as independent signal-transport channels. Consequently, waveguide positioning is primarily exploited to optimize the geometry of free-space radiation, whereas the electromagnetic interactions among the waveguides themselves are typically neglected.
This independent-waveguide assumption is justified when neighboring waveguides are sufficiently far apart. However, it does not generally hold for closely spaced dielectric waveguides. Unlike idealized transmission lines with perfectly confined electromagnetic fields, dielectric waveguides support evanescent fields \cite{yeh2008essence,marcatili1969dielectric} that extend beyond their cores and decay exponentially into the surrounding medium. When adjacent waveguides are brought into close proximity, these fields overlap and induce coherent energy exchange between the guided modes, a phenomenon known as inter-waveguide evanescent coupling \cite{marcatili1969dielectric,marcuse1971coupling}. Such coupling is well understood in electromagnetic waveguide theory, where it is often treated as crosstalk to be suppressed when independent transmission paths are desired \cite{song2015high,mrejen2015adiabatic}. Accordingly, maintaining sufficient waveguide separation provides a convenient way to preserve the independent-waveguide model commonly adopted in PASS. Yet, as waveguide positions become increasingly configurable, enforcing large separations also rules out a potentially useful class of deployment configurations. This motivates a fundamental question: \emph{Must inter-waveguide evanescent coupling be avoided in PASS, or can it be deliberately exploited to improve wireless communication performance?}
Interestingly, the distinctive signal-radiation mechanism of PASS creates an opportunity to exploit such coupling in a way that differs from conventional coupled-waveguide systems. When multiple waveguides are electromagnetically coupled, signals launched into individual waveguides undergo coherent redistribution during propagation, leading to coupling-dependent variations in their amplitudes and phases \cite{marcuse1971coupling,christodoulides2003discretizing}. In PASS, however, these signals are not simply collected at the ends of the waveguides. Instead, multiple PAs sequentially extract portions of the guided signals at different propagation positions \cite{wang2025modeling,11263923}, while the remaining signals continue propagating and interacting with neighboring waveguides. As a result, signals radiated from different PA locations experience different coupling-induced evolutions before reaching the intended receivers. Their subsequent superposition in free space can therefore be either constructive or destructive, depending on the accumulated coupling and propagation phases. This suggests that inter-waveguide coupling, rather than merely introducing unwanted signal leakage, can be harnessed as an additional analog beamforming resource to reshape the effective wireless channels.
However, exploiting this opportunity is far from straightforward. Unlike an active amplification mechanism, evanescent coupling does not create additional signal power; it redistributes existing guided signals. Therefore, stronger coupling does not necessarily improve the received signal strength or achievable rate. The potential benefit instead lies in how coupling modifies the coherent combining of signals radiated from different PA extraction sections. This introduces a new dimension to PASS design: \emph{waveguide spacing not only determines the physical distribution of radiating elements but also controls the coherent evolution of guided signals before radiation}. To translate this physical insight into communication gains, several challenges must be addressed. 
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=0.45cm]
 \item First, a physically grounded channel model is needed to capture the interaction between inter-waveguide coupling, guided propagation, and sequential PA extraction.
 \item Second, the relationship between coupling and communication performance must be established to identify when coupling is beneficial and how waveguide spacing should be designed.
 \item Third, in multi-user scenarios, coupling-aware waveguide deployment must be jointly coordinated with PA placement and digital precoding, since these design variables simultaneously affect coherent signal combining, radiation geometry, and inter-user interference.
\end{itemize}  
In this paper, we address these challenges by developing a comprehensive electromagnetic and communication-theoretic framework for coupled-waveguide pinching-antenna systems (CWPASS). By explicitly incorporating inter-waveguide evanescent coupling into the signal propagation and radiation processes, we establish a direct connection between waveguide electromagnetic interactions and end-to-end wireless communication performance. More importantly, we reveal that the communication benefit of coupling arises from its ability to reshape coherent combining across distributed PA extraction sections, rather than from increasing the available guided signal power. This insight enables the development of coupling-aware waveguide-spacing principles and analytical performance bounds. Building on these results, we further investigate how inter-waveguide coupling can be jointly exploited with spatial reconfiguration and digital beamforming to improve multi-hotspot communication performance.
Our main contributions are summarized as follows.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=0.45cm]
 \item We introduce CWPASS, which harnesses inter-waveguide evanescent coupling as an additional physical degree of freedom for wireless channel reconfiguration. Unlike conventional multi-waveguide PASS, where individual waveguides are modeled as independent transmission paths, CWPASS exploits coherent energy exchange among neighboring waveguides to manipulate the signals radiated by distributed PAs. The key insight is that the interaction between coupled propagation and sequential PA extraction enables coupling-dependent coherent combining, providing a new mechanism for analog beamforming.
 \item Starting from the guided electromagnetic modes of rectangular dielectric waveguides, we characterize evanescent-field penetration and the resulting induced polarization currents, from which analytical inter-waveguide coupling coefficients are derived. We then establish a coupled-propagation model and explicitly incorporate sequential PA extraction and free-space radiation to obtain the end-to-end CWPASS channel. The resulting framework captures the coupling-dependent amplitude and phase evolution of signals radiated from different PA locations.
 \item We investigate how inter-waveguide coupling affects coherent signal combining across PA extraction sections. By decomposing the effective channel gain into power accumulation and inter-section coherent contributions, we identify the mechanism through which coupling influences communication performance. Based on this characterization, we derive closed-form phase-matching-based waveguide-spacing candidates for the two-waveguide case, establish coupling-aware channel-gain and achievable-rate upper bounds, and characterize the rate-ceiling scaling with the number of waveguides and necessary conditions for individual rate guarantees. These results demonstrate why coupling strength alone is insufficient to determine communication performance and provide analytical guidance for coupling-aware deployment.
 \item For multi-hotspot communications, we formulate a sum-rate maximization problem that jointly optimizes waveguide positions, PA locations, and digital precoding under transmit-power, spatial deployment, and individual rate constraints. To address the resulting nonconvex problem, we develop two complementary algorithms based on weighted minimum mean-square error and successive convex approximation (WMMSE-SCA), and signal-domain penalty dual decomposition (PDD), respectively. Numerical results demonstrate that, at 40 dBm, optimizing waveguide positions under negligible coupling improves the sum rate by approximately 39\
\end{enumerate}
\section{System Model}\label{sec:II}

\begin{figure}
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=0.76\linewidth]{figures/system_model.pdf}
 \caption{Illustration of the considered CWPASS, where an access point feeds $M$ coupled single-mode dielectric waveguides, each hosting $N$ PAs, to serve physical agents distributed within multiple hotspots.}
 \label{fig:system_model}
\end{figure}
We consider a coupled-waveguide pinching-antenna system, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:system_model}.
The system comprises $M$ single-mode dielectric waveguides, each connected to an independent radio-frequency chain and hosting $N$ PAs.
The dielectric waveguides and PAs share an identical rectangular cross section with dimensions $a\times b$, where the refractive indices of the waveguide core and cladding are denoted by $n_1$ and $n_0$, respectively.
An internal attenuation coefficient $\alpha_{\mathrm W}$ represents the propagation loss per unit length within the waveguides.
\subsection{Spatial Configuration}
The service region is a 3D volume of size $D_x\times D_y\times D_z$.
Any point in the Cartesian coordinate system is represented by the coordinate tuple $\bm{\psi}=(x,y,z)$.
\subsubsection{Physical Agents (AE)}
The position of the $k$-th physical agent ($k=1,\ldots,K$) is $\bm{\psi}^{(\mathrm{AE})}_k\triangleq\big(x^{(\mathrm{AE})}_k,y^{(\mathrm{AE})}_k,0\big)$.
The $K$ single-antenna physical agents are uniformly distributed within several hotspots in the service region.
\subsubsection{Waveguides (WG)}
The $M$ waveguides are placed horizontally at height $D_z$, oriented parallel to the $x$-axis, with potentially nonuniform spacing along the $y$-axis.
Each waveguide has length $D_x$.
The feed point of the $m$-th waveguide ($m=1,\ldots,M$) is denoted by $\bm{\psi}^{(\mathrm{WG})}_m\triangleq\left(x^{(\mathrm{WG})}_m,y^{(\mathrm{WG})}_m,z^{(\mathrm{WG})}_m\right)$, where $z^{(\mathrm{WG})}_m=D_z$ and $x^{(\mathrm{WG})}_m=0$.
\subsubsection{PAs}
On waveguide $m$, $N$ PAs are deployed.
The $n$-th PA on waveguide $m$ is indexed by $m_n$, and its
radiation center is denoted by
$\bm{\psi}^{(\mathrm{PA})}_{m_n}
\triangleq
\big(
x^{(\mathrm{PA})}_{m_n},
y^{(\mathrm{PA})}_{m_n},
z^{(\mathrm{PA})}_{m_n}
\big)$,
where
$y^{(\mathrm{PA})}_{m_n}=y^{(\mathrm{WG})}_m$
and
$z^{(\mathrm{PA})}_{m_n}=D_z$.
Each PA extracts a fraction of the local guided signal, while
the remaining signal continues to propagate along the coupled
waveguides.
\subsection{Signal Model in CWPASS}
Let $\bm{s}_0\in\mathbb{C}^{K\times1}$ denote the vector of
independent data symbols intended for the $K$ physical agents, with
$\mathbb{E}\{\bm{s}_0\bm{s}_0^{\mathsf H}\}=\bm I_K$.
The digital precoder $\bm W\in\mathbb{C}^{M\times K}$ maps the
$K$ data streams to the $M$ waveguide inputs as
\begin{equation}
\bm s=\bm W\bm s_0,\qquad
\operatorname{tr}(\bm W\bm W^{\mathsf H})\le1,
\label{e:s}
\end{equation}
where the $m$-th entry $s_m$ of $\bm s$ denotes the excitation
launched into the $m$-th waveguide.
After entering the waveguides, the signals undergo
inter-waveguide coupling and distributed PA extraction
before propagating through free space to the physical agents.
Accordingly, the received signal vector
$\bm\xi \in\mathbb{C}^{K\times1}$ is given by
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\bm \xi
&=
\sqrt{P}\bm H\bm W\bm s_0+\bm\nu

&\triangleq
\sqrt{P}
\bm H^{(\mathrm{P}\to\mathrm{A})}
\bm H^{(\mathrm{W}\to\mathrm{P})}
\big(\bm H^{(\mathrm{CP})}\big)
\bm W\bm s_0
+\bm\nu,
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:end_to_end}
\end{equation}
where $P$ is the total transmit power,
$\bm\nu\sim\mathcal{CN}(\bm0,\sigma^2\bm I_K)$ is the
additive white Gaussian noise (AWGN), and
$\bm H\in\mathbb{C}^{K\times M}$ is the effective
end-to-end channel matrix.
The channel model in \eqref{eq:end_to_end} consists of
three components:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=0.4cm]
 \item \textit{Coupled-propagation channel:}
 $\bm H^{(\mathrm{CP})}\in\mathbb{C}^{M\times M}$
 characterizes the signal transfer among the $M$
 waveguides induced by inter-waveguide electromagnetic
 coupling over a given propagation distance.
 \item \textit{Waveguide-to-PA channel:}
 $\bm H^{(\mathrm{W}\to\mathrm{P})}
 \big(\bm H^{(\mathrm{CP})}\big)
 \in\mathbb{C}^{MN\times M}$
 maps the $M$ waveguide inputs to the $MN$ PA outputs,
 accounting for coupled propagation, waveguide attenuation,
 PA locations, and distributed PA extraction.
 \item \textit{PA-to-agent channel:}
 $\bm H^{(\mathrm{P}\to\mathrm{A})}
 \in\mathbb{C}^{K\times MN}$
 describes free-space propagation from the $MN$ PAs
 to the $K$ physical agents and is specified below.
\end{itemize}
We characterize the free-space propagation from the PAs to
the physical agents. Each PA follows a cosine-power radiation
pattern given by \cite{balanis2005antenna}
\begin{equation}
G_{\mathrm{PA}}(\varphi)
=
\begin{cases}
2(\zeta+1)\cos^\zeta\varphi,
& 0\le\varphi\le\pi/2,
0,
& \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}
\label{eq:pa_radiation_pattern}
\end{equation}
where $\zeta\ge0$ controls the radiation directivity and
$\varphi$ denotes the angle measured from the downward boresight.
For notational convenience, we index the $MN$ PAs by
$q=1,\ldots,MN$, with $\bm\psi_q^{(\mathrm{PA})}$
denoting the position of the corresponding PA.
For physical agent $k$ and PA $q$, the corresponding radiation
angle is determined by
\begin{equation}
\cos\varphi_{k,q}
=
\frac{
z_q^{(\mathrm{PA})}
}{
\left\|
\bm\psi_k^{(\mathrm{AE})}
-
\bm\psi_q^{(\mathrm{PA})}
\right\|
}.
\label{eq:pa_user_angle}
\end{equation}
To retain the spherical-wave amplitude and phase variations across
the distributed PAs without invoking a plane-wave approximation,
the corresponding PA-to-agent channel coefficient is modeled as
\begin{equation}
h_{k,q}^{(\mathrm{P}\to\mathrm{A})}
\!=\!
\frac{
\lambda
\sqrt{
G_{\mathrm{PA}}(\varphi_{k,q})
}}{
4\pi
\left\|
\bm\psi_k^{(\mathrm{AE})}
\!-\!
\bm\psi_q^{(\mathrm{PA})}
\right\|
}
e^{
-jk_0
\left\|
\bm\psi_k^{(\mathrm{AE})}
-
\bm\psi_q^{(\mathrm{PA})}
\right\|},
\label{eq:pa_user_channel}
\end{equation}
where $k_0=2\pi/\lambda$ is the free-space wavenumber,
$\lambda=c/f$ is the wavelength at operating frequency $f$,
and $c$ is the speed of light in vacuum.
Collecting the channel coefficients of all PAs, the
PA-to-agent channel matrix is constructed as
\begin{equation}
\bm H^{(\mathrm{P}\to\mathrm{A})}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
h_{1,1}^{(\mathrm{P}\to\mathrm{A})}
&
\cdots
&
h_{1,MN}^{(\mathrm{P}\to\mathrm{A})}

\vdots
&
\ddots
&
\vdots

h_{K,1}^{(\mathrm{P}\to\mathrm{A})}
&
\cdots
&
h_{K,MN}^{(\mathrm{P}\to\mathrm{A})}
\end{bmatrix}
\in\mathbb C^{K\times MN}.
\label{eq:pa_user_channel_matrix}
\end{equation}
Let $\bm h_k^{\mathsf H}$ denote the $k$-th row of $\bm H$,
and let $\bm w_k$ denote the $k$-th column of $\bm W$.
The received signal at physical agent $k$ is
\begin{equation}
\xi_k
=
\sqrt{P}\bm h_k^{\mathsf H}\bm w_k s_{0,k}
+
\sqrt{P}\sum_{i\ne k}
\bm h_k^{\mathsf H}\bm w_i s_{0,i}
+\nu_k,
\label{eq:user_received}
\end{equation}
where the first term is the desired signal and the second
term represents multi-agent interference.
Accordingly, treating multi-agent interference as noise,
the achievable rate of physical agent $k$ is
\begin{equation}
R_k
=
\log_2\!\left(
1+
\frac{
P\left|\bm h_k^{\mathsf H}\bm w_k\right|^2
}{
P\sum_{i\ne k}
\left|\bm h_k^{\mathsf H}\bm w_i\right|^2
+\sigma^2
}
\right).
\label{eq:k_rate}
\end{equation}
The above signal model reveals two key characteristics
of CWPASS:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=0.4cm]
 \item \textit{Coupling-dependent signal propagation:}
 Unlike conventional PASS with independent waveguides,
 CWPASS allows guided signals to exchange energy among
 different waveguides.
 Since coupled propagation and PA extraction occur
 successively along the propagation direction,
 $\bm H^{(\mathrm{CP})}$ is incorporated into the
 waveguide-to-PA channel rather than appearing as
 an independent cascaded channel.
 \item \textit{Coupling-induced analog beamforming:}
 The interaction between inter-waveguide coupling
 and distributed PA extraction determines the
 amplitudes and phases of the signals radiated by
 different PAs.
 This provides an additional means of shaping
 the effective multi-agent channels beyond digital
 precoding at the waveguide inputs.
\end{itemize}
The coupled-propagation and waveguide-to-PA channels
are derived in the next section.

\section{Evanescent Coupling Characterization}\label{sec:III}

In this section, we characterize the evanescent coupling and guided-wave propagation in CWPASS in three steps.
First, we characterize the guided mode and its evanescent field penetrating neighboring dielectric waveguides.
Second, we derive the induced polarization current and project it onto the guided mode of the receiving waveguide to obtain the pairwise inter-waveguide coupling coefficient.
Finally, we establish the coupled-propagation model and incorporate PA extraction to construct the waveguide-to-PA channel of CWPASS.
\subsection{Evanescent Field Penetration}
\label{subsec:guided_evanescent_fields}
We first characterize the electromagnetic field carried by an
individual dielectric waveguide before introducing the interaction
among different waveguides. This provides the physical basis for the
inter-waveguide coupling coefficients derived subsequently.
To characterize the guided electromagnetic field supported by each
dielectric waveguide, we start from the vector wave equation governing
the electric field,
\begin{equation}
\nabla\times\nabla\times\bm{\mathcal E}
-
\omega^2\psi_0\varepsilon(y,z)\bm{\mathcal E}
=
\bm 0 ,
\label{eq:vector_wave_equation}
\end{equation}
where $\omega=2\pi f$ is the angular frequency with $f$ being
the operating frequency, $\psi_0$ and $\varepsilon_0$ are the
vacuum permeability and permittivity, respectively, and
$\varepsilon(y,z)$ describes the dielectric distribution over the
waveguide cross section.
For the $m$-th isolated waveguide, the
rectangular core is $\mathcal S_m\!=\!\{\!(y,z)\!:\!
|y\!-\!y_m^{(\mathrm{WG})}|\!\le \!\frac{a}{2},
|z\!-\!D_z|\!\le\! \frac{b}{2}\!\}$, with permittivity $\varepsilon_0n_1^2$ inside
the core and $\varepsilon_0n_0^2$ in the surrounding cladding.
The dielectric waveguide is uniform along the $x$-direction, and
therefore the permittivity distribution $\varepsilon(y,z)$ is
independent of $x$. Consequently, the wave equation in
\eqref{eq:vector_wave_equation} admits guided-mode solutions with a
separable longitudinal dependence. For the $m$-th isolated
waveguide, the fundamental guided mode can therefore be written as
\begin{equation}
\bm{\mathcal E}_m^{(\mathrm{iso})}(x,y,z)
=
\bm{\mathcal E}_m^{(\mathrm{co})}(y,z)e^{-j\beta_gx},
\label{eq:isolated_guided_mode}
\end{equation}
where $\bm{\mathcal E}_m^{(\mathrm{co})}$ is the transverse modal field and
$\beta_g$ is the corresponding longitudinal propagation constant.
Substituting \eqref{eq:isolated_guided_mode} into
\eqref{eq:vector_wave_equation} reduces the three-dimensional wave
equation to a Maxwell eigenvalue problem over the waveguide cross
section, from which $\bm{\mathcal E}_m^{(\mathrm{co})}$ and $\beta_g$ are
determined.
For an open rectangular dielectric waveguide, the transverse
vector field generally does not admit a simple separable
closed form. We therefore adopt the Marcatili approximation,
under which the fundamental mode has a dominant electric
field component along the $y$-direction, while the weaker
field components in the corner regions are neglected
\cite{marcatili1969dielectric}. The electric field inside
the core of waveguide $m$ is 
\cite{okamoto2021fundamentals}
\begin{equation}
\hspace{-0.2cm}\bm{\mathcal E}_m^{(\mathrm{co})}(y,z)
\!\approx\!
\mathcal E_0
\cos\!\left[\!
k_y\!\left(y\!-\!y_m^{(\mathrm{WG})}\right)\!
\right]
\cos\!\left[
k_z(z\!-\!D_z)
\right]\bm e_y,
\label{eq:core_mode_field}
\end{equation}
where $\bm e_y=(0,1,0)^{\top}$, $(y,z)\in\mathcal S_m$, and $\mathcal E_0$ is the normalization factor under the scalar power convention defined below. The transverse wavenumbers $k_y$ and $k_z$ are parameterized by the slab-based dispersion relations
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:transverse_wavenumber_y}
 \tan\!\left(\frac{k_ya}{2}\right)
\!=\!\frac{n_1^2\alpha_y}{n_0^2k_y}, \quad 
\tan\!\left(\frac{k_zb}{2}\right)
\!=\!
\frac{
\sqrt{(\frac{\omega}{c})^2(n_1^2-n_0^2)-k_z^2}
}{
k_z
},
\end{equation}
where
$\alpha_y\triangleq
\sqrt{(\frac{\omega}{c})^2(n_1^2-n_0^2)-k_y^2}$
is the lateral evanescent decay constant. The dimensionless
quantities $k_ya/2$ and $k_zb/2$ represent the transverse phase
accumulated from the center of the waveguide to the corresponding
core--cladding interface. The longitudinal propagation constant is
$\beta_g=
\sqrt{(\omega n_1/c)^2-k_y^2-k_z^2}$.
\begin{prop}[Unit-Power Modal Normalization]
\label{prop:modal_normalization}
Under the Marcatili approximation, the normalization factor
$\mathcal E_0$ that makes the isolated fundamental mode carry unit
guided power is given by
\begin{align}\label{eq:modal_normalization_factor}
\mathcal E_0=
&\Bigg\{
\frac{2\omega\psi_0}{\beta_g}
\left[
\frac{a}{2}
+\frac{\sin(k_ya)}{2k_y}
+\frac{\cos^2(\frac{k_ya}{2})}{\alpha_y}
\right]^{-1}

\times&
\left[
\frac{b}{2}
+\frac{\sin(k_zb)}{2k_z}
+
\frac{
\cos^2(\frac{k_zb}{2})
}{
\sqrt{
(\frac{\omega}{c})^2(n_1^2-n_0^2)-k_z^2
}
}
\right]^{-1}
\Bigg\}^{1/2}.\nonumber
\end{align}
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
Please see the supplementary material.
\end{proof}
Using the unit-power normalized fundamental mode above, the
guided electric field associated with the excitation
$s_m$ in the $m$-th waveguide is
\begin{equation}
\bm{\mathcal E}_m^{(\mathrm{WG})}(x,y,z)
=
\bm{\mathcal E}_m^{(\mathrm{co})}(y,z)s_m
e^{-\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm W}}{2}x}
e^{-j\beta_gx}.
\label{eq:guided_field_CWPASS}
\end{equation}
Here,
$e^{-\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm W}}{2}x}$ accounts for the internal waveguide
attenuation and $e^{-j\beta_gx}$ represents the guided-wave
phase accumulation.
We next characterize the evanescent field extending from one
waveguide to another.
\begin{thm}[Pairwise Evanescent Field]
\label{thm:pairwise_evanescent_field}
Consider two distinct waveguides $m$ and $m'$, and without loss of
generality assume
$y_{m'}^{(\mathrm{WG})}>y_m^{(\mathrm{WG})}$.
Under the adopted guided-mode model, the incident tail of waveguide $m$, evaluated in the region occupied by core $m'$, is given by
\begin{align}
\label{eq:evanescent_field_mm}
&\bm{\mathcal E}_{m\rightarrow m'}^{(\mathrm{ev})}(x,y,z)
=
\mathcal E_0 s_m
e^{-\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm W}}{2}x}
e^{-j\beta_gx}
\cos\!\left(\frac{k_ya}{2}\right)

&\times
\exp\!\left[
-\alpha_y
\left(
\frac{a}{2}
+y-y_{m'}^{(\mathrm{WG})}
\right)
\right]
\cos\!\left[
k_z(z-D_z)
\right]
\nonumber 
&\times
\exp\!\left[
-\alpha_y
\left(
\left|
y_m^{(\mathrm{WG})}
-
y_{m'}^{(\mathrm{WG})}
\right|-a
\right)
\right] \bm e_y, \quad(y,z)\in\mathcal S_{m'}.\nonumber
\end{align} 
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
Please see the supplementary material.
\end{proof}
As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:evanescent_field},
Theorem~\ref{thm:pairwise_evanescent_field}
shows that the guided field extends into the
surrounding cladding and decays exponentially
along the transverse direction.
Its penetration into the neighboring waveguide
core induces polarization currents, providing
the physical basis for inter-waveguide coupling.
Consequently, the interaction weakens as the
waveguide separation increases.
\subsection{Coupling Coefficient}
\label{subsec:modal_projection_coupling}
The evanescent field derived in
Theorem~\ref{thm:pairwise_evanescent_field} penetrates into the
dielectric core of waveguide $m'$ and induces a polarization
response due to the permittivity contrast between the core and the
surrounding cladding. We first characterize the resulting equivalent
polarization current and then project it onto the fundamental guided
mode of waveguide $m'$ to determine the pairwise coupling
coefficient.
\begin{cor}[Induced Polarization Current]
\label{lem:polarization_current}
The evanescent field of waveguide $m$ induces
the following polarization current density
within the core $\mathcal S_{m'}$:
\begin{equation}
\bm J_{m\rightarrow m'}^{(\mathrm{pol})}(x,y,z)
=
j\omega\varepsilon_0
\left(n_1^2\!-\!n_0^2\right)
\bm{\mathcal E}_{m\rightarrow m'}^{(\mathrm{ev})}(x,y,z),
\label{eq:polarization_current_mm}
\end{equation}
\end{cor}
The polarization current in \eqref{eq:polarization_current_mm}
acts as a distributed source that excites the fundamental mode
of waveguide $m'$ through modal overlap. For the unit-power
normalized mode in \eqref{eq:unit_power_condition}, the forward
excitation amplitude is given by \cite{pozar2021microwave}
\begin{equation}
A_{m\rightarrow m'}
\!=\!
-\frac{1}{4}\!
\iiint_V
\!\left[\!
\bm{\mathcal E}_{m'}^{(\mathrm{iso})}(x,y,z)
\!\right]^*\!\!\!\cdot\!
\bm J_{m\rightarrow m'}^{(\mathrm{pol})}(x,y,z)
dV,\!\!\!
\label{eq:standard_modal_excitation}
\end{equation}
where $V$ denotes the integration volume within the core of
waveguide $m'$, and $A_{m\rightarrow m'}$ denotes the excited
forward fundamental-mode amplitude in waveguide $m'$.
Since the polarization current is distributed along the
$x$-direction, the excitation per unit length becomes
\begin{align}\label{eq:modal_current_projection}
&\frac{dA_{m\rightarrow m'}}{dx}
=
-\frac{1}{4}e^{j\beta_gx}
\iint_{\mathcal S_{m'}}
\left[
\bm{\mathcal E}_{m'}^{(\mathrm{co})}
\right]^*
\cdot
\bm J_{m\rightarrow m'}^{(\mathrm{pol})}
\,dy\,dz

&\overset{(a)}{=}
-j\frac{\omega\varepsilon_0(n_1^2-n_0^2)}{4}
e^{j\beta_gx}
\iint_{\mathcal S_{m'}}
\left[
\bm{\mathcal E}_{m'}^{(\mathrm{co})}
\right]^*
\cdot
\bm{\mathcal E}_{m\rightarrow m'}^{(\mathrm{ev})}
\,dy\,dz, \nonumber
\end{align}
where step (a) follows from
\eqref{eq:polarization_current_mm} under the
dominant-field approximation.
Since $\bm{\mathcal E}_{m\rightarrow m'}^{(\mathrm{ev})}$
contains the longitudinal factor
$s_m e^{-\alpha_{\mathrm W}x/2}e^{-j\beta_gx}$,
the guided-wave phases cancel. We thus define the
coupling coefficient $\mu_{mm'}$ through
\begin{equation}
\frac{dA_{m\rightarrow m'}}{dx}
\triangleq
-j\mu_{mm'}s_m e^{-\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm W}}{2}x},
\label{eq:coupling_coefficient_definition}
\end{equation}
where $\mu_{mm'}$ characterizes the mode conversion from waveguide
$m$ to waveguide $m'$ per unit propagation length.
\begin{figure}
 \centering
 \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{figures/physical.pdf}
 \caption{Evanescent-field interaction between two neighboring dielectric waveguides. }

 \label{fig:evanescent_field}
\end{figure}
\begin{thm}[Pairwise Inter-Waveguide Coupling]
\label{thm:pairwise_coupling}
For two nonoverlapping identical rectangular waveguides, evaluating \eqref{eq:modal_current_projection} with the scalar envelope gives the coefficient model
\begin{align}\label{eq:pairwise_coupling_closed_form}
&\mu_{mm'} \!\left(d_{mm'}^{(\mathrm{WG})}\right)
\!\approx\!
\frac{
|\mathcal E_0|^2\alpha_y
\cos^2\!\left(\!\frac{k_ya}{2}\!\right)
}{
4\omega\psi_0
}
\left(
\frac{b}{2}
\!+\!
\frac{\sin(k_zb)}{2k_z}
\right)

&\times
\left[
1+\frac{n_1^2}{n_0^2}
+
\left(
\frac{n_1^2}{n_0^2}-1
\right)e^{-\alpha_ya}
\right]
\exp\!\left[
-\alpha_y
\left(
d_{mm'}^{(\mathrm{WG})}-a
\right)
\right],\nonumber
\end{align}
where the transverse distance between waveguides $m$ and $m'$ is
defined as
$d_{mm'}^{(\mathrm{WG})}
\triangleq
\left|
y_m^{(\mathrm{WG})}
-
y_{m'}^{(\mathrm{WG})}
\right|$.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}
Without loss of generality, assume
$y_{m'}^{(\mathrm{WG})}>y_m^{(\mathrm{WG})}$.
Substituting \eqref{eq:polarization_current_mm},
\eqref{eq:core_mode_field}, and
\eqref{eq:evanescent_field_mm} into
\eqref{eq:modal_current_projection}
and separating the transverse modal-overlap
integrals yields
\begin{align}\label{eq:coupling_separated_integrals}
\mu_{mm'}
&\!=\!
\frac{
\omega\varepsilon_0(n_1^2\!-\!n_0^2)
|\mathcal E_0|^2
}{4}
\cos\!\left(\!\frac{k_ya}{2}\!\right) \!
e^{-\alpha_y
\left(
\left|
y_m^{(\mathrm{WG})}
\!-y_{m'}^{(\mathrm{WG})}
\right|
-\frac{a}{2}
\right)}
\nonumber 
&\times
\int_{y_{m'}^{(\mathrm{WG})}-\frac{a}{2}}
 ^{y_{m'}^{(\mathrm{WG})}+\frac{a}{2}}
e^{-\alpha_y(y-y_{m'}^{(\mathrm{WG})})}
\cos\!\left[
k_y(y-y_{m'}^{(\mathrm{WG})})
\right]dy
\nonumber 
&\times
\int_{D_z-\frac{b}{2}}^{D_z+\frac{b}{2}}
\cos^2\!\left[k_z(z-D_z)\right]dz .
\end{align}
The vertical integral evaluates to
$b/2+\sin(k_zb)/(2k_z)$, while the lateral
integral is evaluated using the boundary
condition in \eqref{eq:transverse_wavenumber_y}.
Applying
$k_y^2+\alpha_y^2
=(\omega/c)^2(n_1^2-n_0^2)$
and $\psi_0\varepsilon_0=c^{-2}$,
and collecting the exponential terms,
yields \eqref{eq:pairwise_coupling_closed_form}.
\end{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:pairwise_coupling} shows that the coupling
coefficient consists of a common modal factor determined by the
waveguide structure and an exponential factor determined by the
transverse separation between the two waveguides. Therefore, for
fixed waveguide parameters, the inter-waveguide coupling strength
can be directly controlled through the waveguide spacing and decays
exponentially as the separation increases.
\subsection{Coupled Propagation}
\label{subsec:coupled_channel}
Collecting the pairwise coupling coefficients derived in
Theorem~\ref{thm:pairwise_coupling}, the coupling matrix of the
$M$ waveguides is written as
\begin{equation}
\bm C=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & \mu_{12} & \mu_{13} & \cdots & \mu_{1M}
\mu_{12} & 0 & \mu_{23} & \cdots & \mu_{2M}
\mu_{13} & \mu_{23} & 0 & \cdots & \mu_{3M}
\vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots
\mu_{1M} & \mu_{2M} & \mu_{3M} & \cdots & 0
\end{bmatrix},
\label{eq:coupling_matrix}
\end{equation}
where $\mu_{mm'}=\mu_{m'm}$ follows from reciprocity between the
identical waveguides.
Since the waveguide structure is invariant along the
$x$-direction, $\bm C$ is independent of the propagation distance.
For a longitudinal propagation distance $\Delta x$, the
coupled-propagation channel is
\begin{equation}
\bm H^{(\mathrm{CP})}(\Delta x)
=
e^{-(\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm W}}{2}+j\beta_g)\Delta x}
\exp\!\left(-j\bm C\Delta x\right),
\label{eq:coupled_propagation_channel}
\end{equation}
which captures guided-wave attenuation, phase
accumulation, and inter-waveguide energy exchange.
Fig.~\ref{fig:power_map} illustrates the periodic
power exchange between two coupled waveguides.
Increasing the inter-waveguide gap exponentially
weakens coupling and lengthens the power-transfer
period, implying that stronger coupling does not
necessarily yield greater power transfer at a
given propagation distance.
We next incorporate sequential PA extraction.
For PA $m_n$, the coupling length $\tau_{m_n}$
determines the extracted and remaining guided-field
amplitude factors $\sin(\kappa\tau_{m_n})$ and
$\cos(\kappa\tau_{m_n})$, respectively
\cite{wang2025modeling}, where $\kappa$ denotes
the waveguide-to-PA coupling coefficient.
All $MN$ PAs are indexed by $q=1,\ldots,MN$
in ascending longitudinal order,
$x_1^{(\mathrm{PA})}\le\cdots\le
x_{MN}^{(\mathrm{PA})}$.
Each index retains its hosting waveguide and
coupling length $\tau_q$, with the same ordering
applied to the columns of
$\bm H^{(\mathrm{P}\to\mathrm{A})}$.
\begin{thm}[Waveguide-to-PA Channel]
\label{thm:waveguide_to_pa_channel}
With all $MN$ PAs ordered according to their longitudinal
coordinates, let $x_0^{(\mathrm{PA})}=0$.
If the $q$-th PA is located on waveguide $m$, the
corresponding row of the waveguide-to-PA channel is
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&
\left[
\bm H^{(\mathrm{W}\to\mathrm{P})}
\right]_{q,:}
=
\sin(\kappa\tau_q)
\Bigg[
\bm H^{(\mathrm{CP})}
\left(
x_q^{(\mathrm{PA})}
-
x_{q-1}^{(\mathrm{PA})}
\right)

&\qquad\times
\prod_{i=q-1}^{1}
\left[
\bm D_i
\bm H^{(\mathrm{CP})}
\left(
x_i^{(\mathrm{PA})}
-
x_{i-1}^{(\mathrm{PA})}
\right)
\right]
\Bigg]_{m,:},
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:waveguide_to_pa_channel}
\end{equation}
where
$\bm D_i\in\mathbb C^{M\times M}$ is a diagonal matrix
whose diagonal entries are all one except for the entry
corresponding to the waveguide hosting PA $i$, which is
$\cos(\kappa\tau_i)$, i.e.,
\[
\bm D_i
\triangleq
\operatorname{diag}
\left(
1,\ldots,
\cos(\kappa\tau_i),
\ldots,1
\right).
\]
The product in \eqref{eq:waveguide_to_pa_channel} is taken in
descending order from $i=q-1$ to $i=1$.
\end{thm}
