In this section, we characterize the coupling-aware rate bounds of
CWPASS in two steps. First, we investigate coherent combining across
PA extraction sections and establish its dependence on inter-waveguide
coupling. Second, we derive an achievable-rate upper bound and analyze
its scaling with the number of waveguides.
\label{subsec:performance_bound}
\subsection{Inter-Section Coherence}
\label{subsec:inter_section_coherence}
We first characterize the effective channel gain under distributed
PA extraction and identify the inter-section coherent contributions.
We adopt an offline equal-quota extraction design, under which
the PA coupling lengths are predetermined as
$\tau_{m_n}
=\kappa^{-1}\arcsin\sqrt{(N+1-n)^{-1}}$
and require no real-time adjustment
\cite{zhang2026directional}.
To characterize the coherent interaction among distributed PAs,
we adopt a local scale-separation model.
For the $n$-th PA group, let
$\underline{x}_n=\min_m x_{m_n}^{(\mathrm{PA})}$
and
$\overline{x}_n=\max_m x_{m_n}^{(\mathrm{PA})}$.
The groups occupy nonoverlapping short sections satisfying
$\overline{x}_n<\underline{x}_{n+1}$ and
$\|\bm C\|_2(\overline{x}_n-\underline{x}_n)\ll1$.
Accordingly, the additional coupling evolution within each
short section is neglected, while the accumulated coupling
propagation outside these sections is retained.
Substituting the prescribed coupling lengths into
\eqref{eq:waveguide_to_pa_channel} under this approximation yields
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\bm H^{(\mathrm{W}\to\mathrm{P})}
\!\approx \!\frac{1}{\sqrt N}\bm P_x\!\!
\begin{bmatrix}
\!\operatorname{diag}_{m=1}^{M}
\left(
e^{-\left(\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm W}}{2}+j\beta_g\right)
x_{m_1}^{(\mathrm{PA})}}
\right)e^{-j\bm C L_1}\!
\vdots
\!\operatorname{diag}_{m=1}^{M}
\left(
e^{-\left(\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm W}}{2}+j\beta_g\right)
x_{m_N}^{(\mathrm{PA})}}
\right)e^{-j\bm C L_N}\!
\end{bmatrix},
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:waveguide_pa_section_approx}
\end{equation}
where $\bm P_x\in\{0,1\}^{MN\times MN}$ is a permutation
matrix that rearranges the rows from the section-wise
waveguide ordering to the longitudinal PA ordering, and
$L_n=\underline{x}_n-
\sum_{\ell=1}^{n-1}
(\overline{x}_\ell-\underline{x}_\ell)$
denotes the effective coupled-propagation length to the
$n$-th extraction section, excluding the lengths of
preceding extraction sections.
Combining \eqref{eq:waveguide_pa_section_approx} with
\eqref{eq:pa_user_channel}, the effective channel of
physical agent $k$ is given by
\begin{align}
\label{eq:effective_channel_section}
&\bm h_k^{\mathsf H}
=
\frac{\lambda\sqrt{2(\zeta+1)}D_z^{\frac{\zeta}{2}}}
{4\pi\sqrt N}
\sum_{n=1}^{N}\sum_{m=1}^{M}
\frac{
e^{-\left(\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm W}}{2}
+j\beta_g\right)x_{m_n}^{(\mathrm{PA})}}
}{
r_{k,m_n}^{\frac{\zeta+2}{2}}
}
\nonumber
&\times
e^{-jk_0r_{k,m_n}}
\left[e^{-j\bm C L_n}\right]_{m,:}
\triangleq \sum_{n=1}^{N} \bm h_{k,n}^{\mathsf H} e^{-j\bm C L_n}, 
\end{align}\addtocounter{equation}{1}
where
$r_{k,m_n}
=\|\bm\psi_k^{(\mathrm{AE})}
-\bm\psi_{m_n}^{(\mathrm{PA})}\|$
denotes the distance from PA $m_n$ to physical agent $k$.
Taking the squared norm of
\eqref{eq:effective_channel_section}
and exploiting the unitarity of $e^{-j\bm C L_n}$ yields
\eqref{eq:self_cross_coupling_gain}.
The decomposition reveals three key properties:
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Power accumulation:}
Power accumulation depends on propagation attenuation and
PA-to-agent distances, but not on inter-waveguide coupling.
\item \emph{Coherent combining:}
Coupling controls coherent combining across extraction sections
through $e^{-j\bm C(L_n-L_{n'})}$.
For $N=1$, coupling has no effect on the effective channel gain,
whereas multiple sections allow constructive or destructive combining.
\item \emph{Waveguide spacing design:}
Waveguide spacing should be designed to improve inter-section
coherence rather than simply maximize coupling strength.
\end{itemize}
The coherent cross term in
\eqref{eq:self_cross_coupling_gain} also provides a direct
guideline for selecting the waveguide spacing. For the spacing analysis, we neglect the variation of the
non-coupling propagation response within each short extraction
section.
\begin{cor}[Closed-Form Waveguide Spacing]
\label{cor:optimal_waveguide_spacing}
For $M=2$, suppose the coherent contribution in
\eqref{eq:self_cross_coupling_gain} is dominated by
one extraction-section pair $(n,n')$, $n<n'$,
under the symmetric-supermode approximation.
With exponential coupling
$\mu(d)=\mu(a)e^{-\alpha_y(d-a)}$
and propagation distances evaluated at a fixed
reference geometry, the phase-matched
waveguide-spacing candidates are given by
\begin{align}\label{eq:closed_form_waveguide_spacing}
d_p^\star
=&\frac{1}{\alpha_y}\ln\Bigg\{
\mu(a)(L_{n'}-L_n)
\Bigg[
\frac{1}{2}\sum_{m=1}^{2}
\Big(
k_0(r_{k,m_n}-r_{k,m_{n'}})\nonumber
[-1mm]
&\hspace{13mm}
+\beta_g
(x_{m_n}^{(\mathrm{PA})}
-x_{m_{n'}}^{(\mathrm{PA})})
\Big)
+2\pi p
\Bigg]^{-1}
\Bigg\}+a,
\end{align}
where $p\in\mathbb Z$ is chosen such that
the logarithm is well defined and
$d_p^\star$ lies within the feasible
waveguide-spacing interval.
\end{cor}
\begin{proof}
Please see the supplementary material.
\end{proof}
Corollary~\ref{cor:optimal_waveguide_spacing} shows that
waveguide spacing can be selected to align the coupling-induced
phase with the propagation-phase difference between extraction
sections, thereby enhancing coherent combining.
To further bound the inter-section coherence, we consider
$M$ equally spaced waveguides with adjacent spacing $d$,
for which \eqref{eq:pairwise_coupling_closed_form} gives
$C_{mm'}=\mu(d)e^{-\alpha_y(|m-m'|-1)d}$ for $m\ne m'$,
where $\mu(d)$ denotes the adjacent-waveguide coupling coefficient.
\begin{lem}[Bounds on the Coupling-Matrix Norm]
\label{lem:coupling_saturation}
For the equally spaced deployment, the spectral norm of
$\bm C$ satisfies
\begin{align}
\label{eq:coupling_norm_bound}
&\frac{2\mu(d)}{1-e^{-\alpha_y d}}
\left[
1-
\frac{1-e^{-\alpha_y Md}}
{M\left(1-e^{-\alpha_y d}\right)}
\right]
\le
\|\bm C\|_2

&\qquad\le
\frac{\mu(d)}{1-e^{-\alpha_y d}}
\left[
2
-e^{-\alpha_y d
\left\lfloor\frac{M-1}{2}\right\rfloor}
-e^{-\alpha_y d
\left\lceil\frac{M-1}{2}\right\rceil}
\right].\nonumber
\end{align}
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
Please see the supplementary material.
\end{proof}
Fig.~\ref{fig:coupling_norm} illustrates the impact of
waveguide number and spacing on the coupling strength.
The spectral norm remains within the analytical bounds
in \eqref{eq:coupling_norm_bound}.
For a fixed spacing $d$, $\|\bm C\|_2$ increases with
$M$ and gradually saturates toward
\begin{equation*}
\lim_{M\rightarrow\infty}\|\bm C\|_2
=
\frac{2\mu(d)}{1-e^{-\alpha_y d}}.
\end{equation*}
In contrast, increasing $d$ significantly weakens
inter-waveguide coupling due to the exponential decay
of the evanescent field.
We next examine how inter-waveguide coupling affects
coherent combining across extraction sections.
From \eqref{eq:self_cross_coupling_gain}, the effective
channel gain can be expressed as
\begin{align}\label{eq:h_k_norm}
\|\bm h_k\|^2
=&
\sum_{n=1}^{N}\|\bm h_{k,n}\|^2+
2\sum_{1\le n<n'\le N}
\|\bm h_{k,n}\|\|\bm h_{k,n'}\|
\nonumber
&\times
\operatorname{Re}\!\left\{
\frac{
\bm h_{k,n}^{\mathsf H}
e^{j\bm C(L_{n'}-L_n)}
\bm h_{k,n'}
}{
\|\bm h_{k,n}\|\|\bm h_{k,n'}\|
}
\right\}.
\end{align}
The real normalized inner product characterizes the
coherent interaction between two extraction sections.
Its value ranges from $-1$ to $1$, corresponding to
destructive and constructive combining, respectively.
The following proposition establishes an upper bound
on this correlation in terms of the propagation-phase
differences and inter-waveguide coupling.
\begin{prop}[Inter-Section Coherence Bound]
\label{prop:inter_section_coherence}
For $N\ge2$ and $1\le n<n'\le N$, the real normalized
correlation between extraction sections $n$ and $n'$
satisfies
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:inter_section_correlation_bound}
\operatorname{Re}\!\left\{
\frac{
\bm h_{k,n}^{\mathsf H}
e^{j\bm C(L_{n'}-L_n)}
\bm h_{k,n'}
}{
\|\bm h_{k,n}\|\|\bm h_{k,n'}\|
}
\right\}
\le\cos\theta_{k,nn'},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{align}
\label{eq:inter_section_coherence}
\theta_{k,nn'}
\triangleq&
\Bigg[
\min\!\Bigg\{
\frac{\pi}{2},\,
\min_{1\le m\le M}
\arccos\!\Bigg(\cos\!\Big[
k_0(r_{k,m_{n'}}-r_{k,m_n})
\nonumber[-1mm]
&+\beta_g\big(
x_{m_{n'}}^{(\mathrm{PA})}
-x_{m_n}^{(\mathrm{PA})}
\big)
\Big]\Bigg)\Bigg\}
-
\frac{\mu(d)(L_{n'}-L_n)}
{1-e^{-\alpha_y d}} \nonumber 
&\Big[
2-e^{-\alpha_y d
\left\lfloor\frac{M-1}{2}\right\rfloor}
-e^{-\alpha_y d
\left\lceil\frac{M-1}{2}\right\rceil}
\Big]
\Bigg]_+ .
\end{align}
Here, $[t]_+=\max\{t,0\}$, and
$0\le\cos\theta_{k,nn'}\le1$.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, the
normalized correlation before coupling satisfies
\begin{align}
&\frac{
\operatorname{Re}\{
\bm h_{k,n}^{\mathsf H}\bm h_{k,n'}
\}
}{
\|\bm h_{k,n}\|\|\bm h_{k,n'}\|
}
=
\sum_{m=1}^{M}
\frac{
|[\bm h_{k,n}]_m|
|[\bm h_{k,n'}]_m|
}{
\|\bm h_{k,n}\|\|\bm h_{k,n'}\|
}

&\quad\times
\cos\!\Big[
k_0(r_{k,m_{n'}}-r_{k,m_n})
+\beta_g(
x_{m_{n'}}^{(\mathrm{PA})}
-x_{m_n}^{(\mathrm{PA})})
\Big]
\nonumber
&\le\!
\Bigg[\!
\max_{1\le m\le M}
\!\cos\!\Big[
k_0(r_{k,m_{n'}}\!-\!r_{k,m_n})
\!+\!\beta_g(
x_{m_{n'}}^{(\mathrm{PA})}
\!-\!x_{m_n}^{(\mathrm{PA})})
\Big]
\!\Bigg]_+,\nonumber
\end{align}
For nonzero complex vectors, define their
angular separation as
$\langle\bm a,\bm b\rangle
\triangleq
\arccos\big(\frac{\operatorname{Re}\{\bm a^{\mathsf H}\bm b\}}{\|\bm a\|\|\bm b\|}
\big)$.
The coupling-induced angular displacement
is bounded by
\begin{align}
\left\langle
\bm h_{k,n'},
e^{j\bm C(L_{n'}-L_n)}\bm h_{k,n'}
\right\rangle
&\le
\int_0^{L_{n'}-L_n}
\frac{
\left\|
\bm C e^{j\bm Ct}\bm h_{k,n'}
\right\|
}{
\|\bm h_{k,n'}\|
}\,dt
\nonumber
&\le
\|\bm C\|_2(L_{n'}-L_n),
\end{align}
Applying the angular triangle inequality,
the initial correlation bound, and
Lemma~\ref{lem:coupling_saturation} gives
\begin{align}
&\left\langle
\bm h_{k,n},
e^{j\bm C(L_{n'}-L_n)}\bm h_{k,n'}
\right\rangle

&\ge
\Bigg[
\left\langle
\bm h_{k,n},\bm h_{k,n'}
\right\rangle
-
\left\langle
\bm h_{k,n'},
e^{j\bm C(L_{n'}-L_n)}\bm h_{k,n'}
\right\rangle
\Bigg]_+
\nonumber
&\ge
\Big[
\left\langle
\bm h_{k,n},\bm h_{k,n'}
\right\rangle
-\|\bm C\|_2(L_{n'}-L_n)
\Big]_+
\ge\theta_{k,nn'},\nonumber
\end{align}
Taking the cosine yields
\eqref{eq:inter_section_correlation_bound}.
\end{proof}
