\subsection{Signal-Domain PDD}
\label{subsubsec:signal_pdd}
Unlike WMMSE-SCA, which updates PA and waveguide
positions separately, signal-domain PDD enables
a joint spatial update.
We introduce $\tilde{\bm Y}\in\mathbb C^{K\times K}$
subject to
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\bm Y}
=
\sqrt{P}\bm H\bm W .
\label{eq:pdd_consistency}
\end{equation}
The MSE in \eqref{eq:wmmse_mse} becomes
\begin{equation}
e_k
=
\left|1-u_k^*\tilde Y_{kk}\right|^2
+
\sum_{i\ne k}
\left|u_k^*\tilde Y_{ki}\right|^2
+
\sigma^2|u_k|^2 .
\label{eq:pdd_mse}
\end{equation}
Thus, the WMMSE objective and QoS constraints
depend on $\{\bm W,\bm x,\bm y\}$ only through
\eqref{eq:pdd_consistency}.
Using PDD \cite{shi2020pdd}, with dual matrix
$\bm\Lambda\in\mathbb C^{K\times K}$ and penalty
parameter $\rho>0$, the augmented-Lagrangian
objective is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{\rho}
=
\sum_{k=1}^{K}
\left(v_ke_k-\ln v_k\right)
+
\frac{\rho}{2}
\left\|
\tilde{\bm Y}
-\sqrt{P}\bm H\bm W
+\frac{\bm\Lambda}{\rho}
\right\|_{\mathrm F}^{2},
\label{eq:pdd_augmented_objective}
\end{equation}
subject to the constraints in \eqref{prob:wmmse},
with \eqref{eq:wmmse_qos} evaluated using
\eqref{eq:pdd_mse}.
The inner problem alternately updates
$\{u_k,v_k\}$, $\tilde{\bm Y}$, $\bm W$,
and the joint deployment $(\bm x,\bm y)$.
For fixed $\tilde{\bm Y}$, the receiver and weight
updates follow \eqref{eq:wmmse_uv_update},
replacing $\sqrt{P}\bm h_k^{\mathsf H}\bm w_i$
with $\tilde Y_{ki}$.
The $\tilde{\bm Y}$ subproblem is separable
across rows, with unconstrained minimizer
\begin{equation}
[\tilde{\bm Y}]_{k,:}^{\mathrm{unc}}
=
\frac{
2v_ku_k[\bm I_K]_{k,:}
+
\rho\sqrt{P}[\bm H\bm W]_{k,:}
-
[\bm\Lambda]_{k,:}
}{
2v_k|u_k|^2+\rho
}.
\label{eq:pdd_ytilde_update}
\end{equation}
If the QoS constraint is violated, the solution
is projected onto the feasible MSE ball centered
at $[\bm I_K]_{k,:}/u_k^*$ with radius
$\sqrt{(1+\ln v_k-R_0\ln2)/(v_k|u_k|^2)-\sigma^2}$,
obtained by completing the square in
\eqref{eq:pdd_mse}.
With $\tilde{\bm Y}$ and the spatial deployment fixed,
the precoder update reduces to constrained
least squares:
\begin{equation}
\bm W
=
\left(
P\bm H^{\mathsf H}\bm H
+\varrho\bm I_M
\right)^{\dagger}
\sqrt{P}\bm H^{\mathsf H}
\left(
\tilde{\bm Y}
+\frac{\bm\Lambda}{\rho}
\right),
\label{eq:pdd_precoder_update}
\end{equation}
where $(\cdot)^\dagger$ denotes the Moore--Penrose
pseudoinverse, and $\varrho\ge0$ enforces the
transmit-power constraint and is obtained by
bisection when the constraint is active.
To jointly update the PA and waveguide positions,
let $\bm J_x$ and $\bm J_y$ denote the Jacobians of
$\operatorname{vec}(\sqrt{P}\bm H\bm W)$ with respect
to $\bm x$ and $\bm y$, respectively.
Linearizing around the current deployment gives
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\min_{\Delta\bm x,\Delta\bm y}\quad&
\Bigg\|
\operatorname{vec}\!\left(
\tilde{\bm Y}
-\sqrt{P}\bm H\bm W
+\frac{\bm\Lambda}{\rho}
\right)
-\bm J_x\Delta\bm x
-\bm J_y\Delta\bm y
\Bigg\|_2^2

&+
\chi_x\|\Delta\bm x\|_2^2
+\chi_y\|\Delta\bm y\|_2^2
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:pdd_joint_geometry_update}
\end{equation}
subject to \eqref{e:P1c}--\eqref{e:P1f},
where $\chi_x,\chi_y>0$ are regularization parameters.
The full channel in \eqref{eq:end_to_end}
is recomputed after each spatial update.
After solving the inner problem, the consistency
error in \eqref{eq:pdd_consistency} is evaluated.
If sufficiently small, the dual matrix is updated as
$\bm\Lambda\leftarrow
\bm\Lambda+
\rho(\tilde{\bm Y}-\sqrt{P}\bm H\bm W)$;
otherwise, $\rho$ is increased.
The iterations continue until the consistency
error and objective variation satisfy the
stopping criteria.

\section{Numerical Results}\label{sec:VI}

We consider a service region of $20\times20\times3~\mathrm{m}^3$ at $28~\mathrm{GHz}$.
A compact coupled-waveguide array with $M=6$ dielectric waveguides is deployed, with each waveguide carrying $N=3$ PAs.
The dielectric waveguides have a rectangular cross section of $6.0\times4.0~\mathrm{mm}^2$, with refractive indices
$n_1=1.5$ and $n_0=1$ for the core and cladding, respectively.
The total input power and noise power are set to $20~\mathrm{dBm}$ and $-80~\mathrm{dBm}$, respectively, while the in-waveguide loss is $0.08~\mathrm{dB/m}$.
The cosine-power radiation exponent is set to $\zeta=3$, and the minimum-rate requirement is $R_0=1~\mathrm{bit/s/Hz}$. 
A total of $K=6$ physical agents are randomly generated within two circular hotspots of radius $1.5~\mathrm{m}$, centered at $(6,4,0)$ and $(14,6,0)$, respectively, with three physical agents in each hotspot and the minimum separation $d_{kk'}^{(\mathrm{AE})}=0.5~\mathrm{m}$.
\subsection{Single-Agent Spatial Rate Analysis}
\label{subsec:spatial_rate}
We first examine the spatial rate characteristics of CWPASS
and the tightness of the analytical upper bound.
Consider two coupled waveguides with $N=3$ PAs each,
centered at $y=10$ m with spacing $d=12$ mm,
and PA sections at $x=9.7$, $10$, and $10.3$ m.
A single physical agent moves across the service region
while the waveguide and PA positions remain fixed.
Fig.~\ref{fig:spatial_rate} compares the achievable rate
with the analytical upper bound.
Fig.~\ref{fig:rate_x_cut} reveals pronounced rate
oscillations caused by constructive and destructive
combining among distributed PAs, while the upper bound
captures the potential coherent gain.
Fig.~\ref{fig:rate_y_cut} exhibits an approximately
symmetric profile with two off-center peaks,
indicating that shorter propagation distances
do not necessarily yield higher rates.
The spatial rate map in Fig.~\ref{fig:rate_spatial_map}
further reveals dense peaks and nulls, highlighting
the sensitivity of coherent combining to agent location.
These results demonstrate the joint influence of
propagation geometry and inter-section phase relationships
on spatial rate performance.
