The achievable sum rate increases with both transmit power
and the number of waveguides, as additional waveguides
provide more RF chains and radiating PAs, thereby
increasing the available spatial degrees of freedom.
Both algorithms achieve comparable performance, with
PDD generally yielding slightly higher sum rates
in most of the considered scenarios.
Unless otherwise specified, PDD is employed for the
remaining numerical evaluations.
\subsection{CWPASS Performance}
We now evaluate the performance of the proposed CWPASS framework, focusing on the advantages offered by inter-waveguide coupling and spatial reconfigurability. 
To assess these advantages, we consider the following five system configurations:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin = 0.5cm]
 \item \textbf{CWPASS:} The proposed coupled-waveguide PASS,
 where the waveguide positions and PA locations are jointly
 optimized to exploit inter-waveguide coupling.
 \item \textbf{ES-CWPASS:} Equally spaced CWPASS, where
 adjacent waveguides maintain identical spacing, while
 the common spacing, array center, and PA locations
 remain continuously optimizable.
 \item \textbf{DS-ES-CWPASS:} Discrete-spacing equally
 spaced CWPASS, where the common waveguide spacing is selected
 from the feasible candidates motivated by the
 two-waveguide phase-matching rule in
 Corollary~\ref{cor:optimal_waveguide_spacing},
 with $p\in\{1,\ldots,20\}$ under a fixed reference
 geometry. For each candidate, the array center,
 PA locations, and digital precoder are jointly optimized.
 \item \textbf{MS-PASS:} Minimum-spacing PASS, where
 the waveguide positions and PA locations are jointly
 optimized subject to a minimum adjacent-waveguide
 spacing of $D_{\mathrm{min}}^{(\mathrm{WG})}=0.5$ m \cite{ding2026movable}, effectively suppressing
 inter-waveguide coupling.
 \item \textbf{UF-PASS:} Conventional PASS with waveguides
 uniformly distributed along the $y$-direction of the
 service region. The waveguide positions are fixed,
 whereas the PA locations remain adjustable.
 \item \textbf{MIMO:} A conventional hybrid MIMO system
 with a BS located at $(D_x/2,0,D_z)$, equipped with
 $MN$ antenna elements arranged in a half-wavelength-spaced
 planar array in the $x$-$z$ plane and $M$ RF chains.
\end{itemize}
\subsubsection{Sum Rate vs. Transmit Power}
Fig.~\ref{fig:r_t} presents the achievable sum rate versus
transmit power for the proposed CWPASS and benchmark schemes.
We have the following main observations.
