\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=0.4cm]
\item The sum rates of all schemes increase with
transmit power, with CWPASS consistently achieving
the highest performance.
At $P=40$ dBm, CWPASS achieves approximately
$102$ bps/Hz, compared with $92$, $89$, $86$,
$62$, and $48$ bps/Hz for ES-CWPASS,
DS-ES-CWPASS, MS-PASS, UF-PASS, and MIMO,
respectively.
Moreover, CWPASS closely approaches its
deployment-dependent upper bound of approximately
$105$ bps/Hz.
\item CWPASS outperforms ES-CWPASS by approximately
$10$ bps/Hz, while ES-CWPASS further outperforms
DS-ES-CWPASS by $3$ bps/Hz.
This demonstrates the benefits of nonuniform
waveguide deployment and continuous spacing
optimization.
Nevertheless, DS-ES-CWPASS achieves competitive
performance using the finite phase-matching-based
spacing candidates derived from
Corollary~\ref{cor:optimal_waveguide_spacing}.
\item MS-PASS outperforms UF-PASS by approximately
$24$ bps/Hz.
Since inter-waveguide coupling is negligible
in both schemes, this gain primarily stems from
waveguide position optimization, which enables
the radiating PAs to approach clustered AEs
and reduce propagation loss.
\item CWPASS further outperforms MS-PASS by
approximately $16$ bps/Hz.
Unlike MS-PASS, CWPASS allows closely spaced
waveguides, enabling compact deployment and
exploitable evanescent-field coupling.
This demonstrates that inter-waveguide coupling,
rather than being merely an unwanted interaction,
can provide an additional degree of freedom
for coherent signal combining and wireless
channel reconfiguration.
\item All PASS schemes outperform conventional
MIMO, with even UF-PASS achieving a gain of
approximately $14$ bps/Hz, while CWPASS
provides a gain of $54$ bps/Hz.
This highlights the advantages of distributed
and adjustable PA radiation over fixed
antenna arrays, particularly in multi-hotspot
communication scenarios.
\end{itemize}
\subsubsection{Required Transmit Power vs. QoS Target}
Fig.~\ref{fig:qos_power} illustrates the required transmit
power versus the per-agent QoS target $R_0$, with
waveguide deployment, PA placement, and digital precoding
reoptimized for each target.
The required power increases with $R_0$ for all schemes,
with CWPASS consistently achieving the lowest power
consumption.
As highlighted in the inset, ES-CWPASS and DS-ES-CWPASS
closely approach CWPASS, whereas MS-PASS requires
higher power but still outperforms UF-PASS.
This demonstrates the benefits of flexible waveguide
deployment even under minimum-spacing constraints.
Moreover, the substantial power advantage over
conventional MIMO highlights the effectiveness of
distributed PA radiation, while the additional gains
of CWPASS reflect the potential of compact deployment
and inter-waveguide coupling.
