\section{Conclusion}
This paper turns inter-waveguide evanescent coupling, conventionally regarded as undesirable crosstalk, into an exploitable beamforming resource for PASS. Beyond the resulting communication gains, this finding challenges the conventional view that waveguides should be electromagnetically isolated to ensure efficient signal delivery. Instead, it suggests that the waveguide infrastructure itself can participate in wireless channel shaping, opening a new dimension for communication system design beyond conventional antenna placement and digital precoding. Importantly, the findings do not imply that stronger coupling is always preferable, but rather that electromagnetic interactions should be engineered to serve communication objectives. This perspective offers a promising path toward more spectrally efficient wireless architectures through the joint design of guided-wave propagation and free-space radiation.
Realizing this potential in practical systems calls for further investigation into full-wave electromagnetic validation, hardware implementation, and robustness against fabrication imperfections and deployment uncertainties. Extending coupling-aware design to broadband transmission and dynamic wireless environments would further enable adaptive exploitation of electromagnetic interactions. Ultimately, such advances could transform PASS from a spatially reconfigurable antenna architecture into a more deeply integrated electromagnetic communication platform, where the transmission infrastructure itself becomes an active design resource for wireless performance enhancement.
