\subsection{Simulation Settings}
For training, we set $G_{\min}=-60^\circ$, $G_{\max}=60^\circ$, $\Delta_{\min}=2^\circ$, $\Delta_{\max}=60^\circ$, $\Delta_d=2^\circ$, and $\eta=1^\circ$. The benchmark methods use their best-performing configurations (mini-batch size of 32, gradient threshold of 1, and L2 regularization of $10^{-3}$) and are trained on the SNR ranges reported in their original works to ensure faithful reproduction of their best performance. The BFDNN follows the architecture in Fig.~\ref{fig_4}, with an input of size $3\times\frac{N_t(N_t-1)}{2}=360$ (real, imaginary, and phase parts of the upper-triangular covariance elements) and an output of 64 neurons, corresponding to the real and imaginary parts of the 32 complex HAD beamforming weights. All layers use `He' initialization, a gradient threshold of 8, L2 regularization of $10^{-4}$, and a mini-batch size of 36. All DL models are trained using the Adam optimizer with an initial learning rate of $10^{-3}$, reduced by a factor of 0.7 under a piecewise schedule every 10 epochs, for at most 500 epochs, with validation every 100 iterations and early stopping at a patience of 10. All methods are evaluated under identical test conditions with targets located at $\theta_1=-10.11^\circ$ and $\theta_2=26.11^\circ$.
\subsection{Indirect Direction Estimation from Beam Patterns}
\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=3.0in]{J1_RMSESNR_COMP_SINE_1.eps}
\caption{RMSE vs SNR when the signals are sine waves.}
\label{fig_5}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=3.0in,height=2.1in]
{J1_DOA_LFM_24_03.eps}
\caption{RMSE vs SNR when the signals are LFM waves.}
\label{fig_6}
\end{figure}
The proposed BFDNN is designed to predict beamforming weights rather than target directions. However, target directions can be indirectly estimated from the resulting radiation pattern by locating the dominant beam peaks. To assess this capability, the angular locations of the principal lobes are used to estimate target directions and the corresponding root-mean-square error (RMSE) is compared with dedicated DoA-estimation approaches.
The DoA estimation accuracy of all the methods are compared in Fig. \ref{fig_5} when basic sine waves are considered as signals. Here  classification-DNN approach showcase good performance when compared to all other methods at higher SNRs from -6dB. Our proposed method shows the very next best approach compared to other methods.The classification method demonstrates high RMSE at low SNR levels (below $-10$ dB), where noise dominates and reduces feature separability. As the SNR increases beyond $-6$ dB, a sharp drop in RMSE is observed, indicating a transition from noise-limited to signal-dominant conditions. This abrupt change reflects the thresholding behavior of classification models, wherein performance improves significantly once the input features become sufficiently distinguishable.
Fig. \ref{fig_6} shows the RMSE performance as a function of SNR for LFM signals. It can be noted that, accuracy of all  methods except LDNN are improved. Although BFDNN does not explicitly perform DoA estimation, it achieves substantially lower RMSE than MUSIC, RDNN, and LDNN over all the evaluated SNR range. The Classification-DNN approach provides the lowest RMSE, while the proposed method exhibits competitive performance. The classification method demonstrates high RMSE at very low SNR level (below -30dB), where noise dominates and reduces feature separability. As the SNR increases beyond that, a sharp drop in RMSE is observed, indicating a transition
from noise-limited to signal-dominant conditions. This abrupt change reflects the thresholding behavior of classification models, wherein performance improves significantly once the input features become sufficiently distinguishable. At very low SNR around -30dB the proposed method provides lowest RMSE than all the methods compared. These results indicate that the beamforming solutions generated by BFDNN preserve useful directional information that can be exploited for target localization when required.
The algorithms that rely on grid search like MUSIC and classification- DNN, will impose a lower bound on estimation accuracy when the true angles lies off grid\cite{ref59}. This lower bound is dependent on the grid size. Therefore increase in SNR will not decrease RMSE, when the minimum error is reached in these cases \cite{ref60}.
In our proposed work, the angle is estimated from the radiation pattern by locating the peaks. So the minimum error to be obtained depends on the angular grid resolution. Here, the span $[-60^\circ, 60^\circ]$ is divided into 360 points. Consequently, the RMSE saturates at this grid-limited floor, which explains its flattening at high SNRs.
