Based on the angles estimated using different methods beam forming weights are estimated using the SVD method mentioned in \cite{ref1}. The beampatterns obtained from those weights are plotted in Fig.\ref{fig_7} and Fig.\ref{fig_8} at 10 dB SNR for sine and LFM signals respectively. BFDNN directly predicts beamforming weights from covariance matrix. Eventhough in Fig. \ref{fig_7}, one of the mainlobes gain is less for BFDNN compared to other methods, in practical scenarios when using LFM signals radiation pattern obtained is almost as same as the other methods. As observed in Fig. \ref{fig_8}, the proposed method produces dominant main lobes near the desired target directions and exhibits beam patterns that are qualitatively similar to those obtained using the benchmark approaches. The LDNN based pipeline shows noticeable beam-pointing errors, while all other approaches generate beams in the intended directions. These results indicate that the proposed covariance-to-beamformer mapping captures sufficient spatial information to reproduce the desired beamforming behavior in the considered ISAC scenario.
The accurate beam placement achieved by BFDNN explains the strong indirect DoA-estimation performance observed in Fig. 4. Since target directions correspond to dominant beam peaks, the generated radiation pattern inherently contains directional information even though the network is trained only for beamforming-weight prediction.
\subsection{Effective Spectral Efficiency}
The performance of the downlink communication is evaluated by computing the  Effective Spectral Efficiency (ESE) for highly mobile scenarios \cite{ref61} as: 
\begin{align*} R_{\mathrm {eff}}=&~\left ({1-\frac {T_{\mathrm {tr}}}{T_{\mathrm {B}}}}\right) \times {R_{DL}} \tag{7}\end{align*}
where $T_{B}$ is the beam coherence time  which  represent the average time over which the beams stay aligned and first $T_{tr}$ time of every beam coherence time is allocated for the channel training/beamforming design. $R_{DL}$ is the downlink spectral efficiency (SE) \cite{ref1}.
