\section{Conclusion}
In this letter, we proposed BFDNN, a covariance-to-beamformer learning framework for ISAC systems with reduced beamforming latency. Unlike the conventional two-stage pipeline that estimates the DoAs before generating the beamformer weights, the proposed framework directly predicts the hybrid beamforming weights from covariance-matrix  using a compact regression network, BFDNN. Simulation results show that the generated beam patterns are similar to those of the benchmark DoA-estimation-based approaches while achieving higher ESE in the considered ISAC scenario. The proposed framework also enables indirect direction estimation from the generated beam pattern  with an RMSE lower than that of the MUSIC, RDNN, and LDNN based approaches and competitive with that of the classification-based method, which achieves the lowest RMSE. The main advantage of the framework is its reduced online latency: BFDNN lowers the beamforming prediction time from approximately $3.3\times10^{-3}$--$3.9\times10^{-3}$~s for the benchmark methods to $1.313\times10^{-4}$~s, a reduction of roughly 25--30 times on the evaluated platform. These results indicate that direct covariance-to-beamformer learning is a promising approach for low-latency beam management in future ISAC systems, particularly where rapid beam adaptation is required. Future work includes designing similar low-complexity architectures to extend the framework to mobility scenarios. 
