Fig.~\ref{fig:initial_configuration_sensitivity} evaluates initialization sensitivity using \(\rho_{\rm EE}\triangleq\eta_{\rm EE}(\mathbf R^0,\mathbf Z^0)/\eta_{\rm EE,0}\), where \(\eta_{\rm EE,0}\) corresponds to the reference initialization in Section~\ref{subsec:simulation_setup}. Herein, the CDFs concentrate around \(\rho_{\rm EE}=1\); provides representative, although not uniformly optimal, performance. Randomizing only \(\mathbf Z^0\) causes the smallest variation since pattern switching incurs relatively lower overhead and the subsequent \(\mathbf Z\)-update effectively corrects unfavorable states. Conversely, randomizing only \(\mathbf R^0\) produces a wider distribution by altering both channel and movement cost. Its close overlap with the jointly randomized case confirms that sensitivity is dominated by \(\mathbf R^0\). The narrower distributions for \(N_{\rm RF}=8\) further indicate that additional RF-domain flexibility mitigates unfavorable initialization, though its higher $P_{\rm circ}$ does not improve EE.
\subsection{Performance Comparisons Under Various Scenarios}
\label{subsec:performance_comparisons}
\begin{figure*}[t]
 \centering
 \subfloat[]{
 \includegraphics[width=0.24\textwidth]{Fig/ee_pmax} \label{fig:ee_vs_pmax}}


 \subfloat[]{
 \includegraphics[width=0.24\textwidth]{Fig/ee_ptrans} \label{fig:ee_vs_actual_power}}
 \subfloat[]{
 \includegraphics[width=0.24\textwidth]{Fig/ee_S} \label{fig:ee_vs_num_patterns}}
 \subfloat[]{
 \includegraphics[width=0.24\textwidth]{Fig/ee_Tc} \label{fig:ee_vs_coherence_time}}
 \caption{$\eta_{\rm EE}/W$ versus (a) $P_{\max}$ (b) the actual transmit power $\|\mathbf F_{\rm BB}\|_2$ (c) $S$ and (d) $T_c$.}
 \label{fig:ee_vs_p}
\end{figure*}
\begin{figure*}[t]
 \centering
 \subfloat[]{
 \includegraphics[width=0.24\textwidth]{Fig/ee_N} \label{fig:ee_vs_N}}


 \subfloat[]{
 \includegraphics[width=0.24\textwidth]{Fig/ee_K} \label{fig:ee_vs_K}}


 \subfloat[]{
 \includegraphics[width=0.24\textwidth]{Fig/ee_L} \label{fig:ee_vs_L}}


 \subfloat[]{
 \includegraphics[width=0.24\textwidth]{Fig/ee_Alambda22} \label{fig:ee_vs_normalized_aperture}}
 \caption{$\eta_{\rm EE}/W$ versus (a) $N$ and (b) $K$ (c) $L$ and (d) $A/\lambda$.}
 \label{fig:ee_vs_nk}
\end{figure*}
Fig.~\ref{fig:ee_vs_pmax} compares the bandwidth-normalized EE, \(\eta_{\rm EE}/W\) versus \(P_{\max}\). As shown, \(\eta_{\rm EE}/W\) initially increases since the SE gain outweighs the additional PA consumption, but saturates since the EE-optimal transmit power is reached and \(\|\mathbf F_{\rm BB}\|_F^2\leq P_{\max}\) becomes inactive. The proposed TH-MPRA framework consistently achieves the highest \(\eta_{\rm EE}/W\); its gain over TH-PRA demonstrates the net benefit of optimizing $\mathbf R$, even after accounting for $E_{\rm mov}$. The observed improvement therefore indicates that the additional spatial DoF can be effectively exploited to improve \(\eta_{\rm EE}/W\) despite the associated movement energy overhead. Meanwhile, the larger separation from HBF-MA and TH-FPA further highlights the effectiveness of adapting $\mathbf Z$. In addition, \(N_{\rm RF}=4\) outperforms \(N_{\rm RF}=8\) since the additional DAC and RF-chain power consumption exceeds the corresponding SE gain, demonstrating that greater RF-domain flexibility does not necessarily improve \(\eta_{\rm EE}/W\).
Fig.~\ref{fig:ee_vs_actual_power} further reveals the EE-power tradeoff by plotting $\eta_{\rm EE}/W$ against the actual transmit power $\|\mathbf F_{\rm BB}\|_F^2$. Unlike Fig.~\ref{fig:ee_vs_pmax}, each curve is unimodal; $\eta_{\rm EE}/W$ first increases since additional $\|\mathbf F_{\rm BB}\|_F^2$ substantially improves the achievable SEs, reaches its maximum at an intermediate power level, and then decreases as the PA energy grows approximately linearly whereas the SE increases only logarithmically. The peak for $N_{\rm RF}=8$ occurs at a slightly higher $\|\mathbf F_{\rm BB}\|_F^2$ than $N_{\rm RF}=4$, since a larger radiated power is required to amortize the higher fixed circuit-power expenditure of the additional RF chains; nevertheless, its maximum $\eta_{\rm EE}/W$ remains lower.  The proposed TH-MPRA framework retains a clear advantage over the baselines, since $\mathbf Z$-update concentrates the radiated energy along favorable multipath directions, while $\mathbf R$-update provides a further channel-conditioning gain.
Fig.~\ref{fig:ee_vs_num_patterns} illustrates the effect of enlarging $ \overline{\boldsymbol{\psi}}(\boldsymbol{\Omega})$. As shown, $\eta_{\rm EE}/W$ of the proposed TH-MPRA framework increases noticeably when $S$ grows from 8 to 16, since a richer codebook provides finer angular selectivity and increases the probability of finding $\overline{\psi}_s(\boldsymbol{\Omega}) $ that reinforces the favorable multipath components while suppressing interference.  The improvement becomes marginal beyond $S=32$, however, since the dominant angular regions have already been covered~\cite{Zheng2025TriHybrid, Wang2025ERFAS, tapbeamp, ttbd}, leads to a clear diminishing return. By construction, HBF-MA and TH-FPA do not optimize $\mathbf Z$ and therefore remain essentially invariant with $S$. The persistent gap between the proposed TH-MPRA framework and TH-PRA represents the complementary gain obtained from $\mathbf R$-update. 
These observations suggest that even a moderate $S=16$ or $32$ offers an attractive performance-search-complexity tradeoff.
Fig.~\ref{fig:ee_vs_coherence_time} illustrates how \(T_c\) affects the cost effectiveness. Herein, $\eta_{\rm EE}/W$ of the proposed TH-MPRA and HBF-MA increases with \(T_c\) since $T_{\rm mov}$ and $E_{\rm mov}$ are 
amortized over a longer data interval. In contrast, TH-PRA varies only slightly since $T_{\rm pat}$ is much smaller than $T_{\rm mov}$ herein (\(T_{\rm pat}=0.86\)~ms vs. \(T_{\rm mov}\approx10.2\)~ms), whereas TH-FPA remains constant since it incurs no reconfiguration overhead. TH-MPRA nevertheless achieves the highest $\eta_{\rm EE}/W$ even for short $T_c$, indicating that its joint optimization gain compensates for the associated overhead. Moreover, the persistent advantage of \(N_{\rm RF}=4\) shows that increasing \(T_c\) does not offset $P_{\rm circ}$.
Fig.~\ref{fig:ee_vs_N} shows how $N$ affects $\eta_{\rm EE}/W$. When $N$ increases from 9 to 16, the array gain improves the effective channel strength and interference-suppression capability, yielding a clear increase in $\eta_{\rm EE}/W$. Beyond this, however, the achievable-SE gain exhibits diminishing returns, whereas $P_{\rm circ}$ continues to grow with $N$. Consequently, the proposed TH-MPRA framework, though it maintains superior performance over baselines, attains their largest $\eta_{\rm EE}/W$ around $N=16$ and then gradually decreases, and so is TH-PRA. This degradation is more pronounced for HBF-MA and TH-FPA since they cannot use pattern reconfiguration to extract sufficient channel gain from the enlarged $N$. The result therefore reveals an $\eta_{\rm EE}$-optimal $N$ rather than an ``as-large-as-possible'' rule.
Fig.~\ref{fig:ee_vs_K} depicts the effect of $K$.\footnote{For $N_{\rm RF}=4$, only $K\leq4$ is shown due to $K\leq N_{\rm RF}$~\cite{hbyx, madc}.}  As shown, $\eta_{\rm EE}/W$ increases with $K$ since the spatial multiplexing delivers more aggregate information during the same coherence block and amortizes $P_{\rm circ}$ and reconfiguration costs over a larger SE.  The increase becomes progressively smaller at large $K$ owing to stronger multi-user interference, transmit-power sharing, and the finite DoF. The proposed TH-MPRA framework consistently outperforms the benchmarks, with the gap over TH-PRA reflecting the additional channel orthogonalization provided by position control and the larger gaps over HBF-MA and TH-FPA demonstrating the importance of pattern-domain interference management. Over $K=2-4$, $N_{\rm RF}=4$ achieves higher $\eta_{\rm EE}/W$ because of its lower circuit-power expenditure. 
Fig.~\ref{fig:ee_vs_L} demonstrates that richer multipath is beneficial but produces only a moderate improvement on $\eta_{\rm EE}/W$.  As $L$ increases, the additional directions provide greater spatial diversity~\cite{hjdrl, Zheng2025TriHybrid}, allowing TH-MPRA to jointly select favorable $\mathbf R$ and $\mathbf Z$. The gain gradually saturates since the channel model normalizes the path powers with respect to $L$, hence additional paths increase diversity rather than the received power; moreover, newly added paths can be angularly correlated, or interference generating. Nevertheless, the proposed TH-MPRA framework maintains the largest $\eta_{\rm EE}/W$ throughout, with its persistent gains over TH-PRA, HBF-MA, and TH-FPA highlighting the benefits of jointly exploiting reconfiguration of $\mathbf R$ and $\mathbf Z$, which provides a complementary phase-domain DoF even in rich scattering scenarios.
Fig.~\ref{fig:ee_vs_normalized_aperture} illustrates the effect of \(A/\lambda\). Herein, 
the result reflects the combined effects of array geometry and, for frameworks with position adaptation, antenna movement. 
For TH-MPRA, increasing \(A/\lambda\) from 0.6 to 0.8-0.9 improves \(\eta_{\rm EE}/W\), as the expanded array geometry and positioning flexibility can enhance spatial phase diversity and user separation. As \(A/\lambda\) approaches 1, however, it yields a slightly lower EE. Larger positioning regions can also entail longer movement distances, increasing \(T_{\rm mov}\) and \(E_{\rm mov}\), although their contribution cannot be isolated from the accompanying geometry changes in this experiment. Accordingly, \(A/\lambda=\)0.8-0.9 provides the most favorable balance under the considered setup. Throughout the evaluated range, TH-MPRA consistently outperforms the baselines by jointly exploiting position and pattern adaptation.
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
In this paper, we proposed an energy-efficient multi-user framework with tri-hybrid MPRA. A hardware-inspired power and latency model captured circuit consumption, mechanical and pattern actuation, and transmission-time overhead. A unified AO framework addressed the coupled EE maximization through block-specific optimization techniques, guaranteeing a nondecreasing and convergent EE sequence. Simulations demonstrated performance close to exhaustive search and consistent EE gains over the baselines. These highlight the potential of joint spatial and pattern reconfiguration on MA for energy-efficient wireless network in 6G and beyond.
