\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}
Congenital heart disease (CHD) affects approximately 9 per 1,000
live births~\cite{vanderLinde2011Birth,Liu2019Global} and is a
leading cause of infant morbidity and mortality~\cite{Carvalho2023ISUOG,GBD2020Burden}.
Prenatal detection
supports delivery planning at centers with neonatal cardiac
care~\cite{Holland2015Prenatal}, but detection rates vary widely.
For critical CHD, reported rates range from 13\
international screening programs~\cite{Bakker2019Prenatal}
and from 12\
cardiac surgery~\cite{Quartermain2015Variation}. Including
outflow-tract and three-vessel views alongside the four-chamber
view improves detection~\cite{Donofrio2014Diagnosis,Carvalho2023ISUOG}.
Nevertheless, lesions requiring these additional views are
less consistently detected, and performance depends on examiner
training~\cite{Quartermain2015Variation,Donofrio2014Diagnosis}.
Software has been developed to support prenatal diagnosis
from ultrasound findings~\cite{Abdelnaby2017SonoCAD}, while
artificial intelligence (AI) offers potential support for
CHD detection from ultrasound images. Models that identify
standard planes in freehand fetal ultrasound~\cite{Baumgartner2017SonoNet}
and detect CHD from cardiac
views~\cite{Arnaout2021Ensemble} rely on identifying relevant
frames within ultrasound studies. Developing and evaluating
these models requires expert-labeled cardiac views, ideally
accompanied by image-quality annotations to identify
diagnostically useful frames~\cite{Arnaout2021Ensemble,Fiorentino2023Review}.
However, fetal echocardiography datasets with both frame-level
view and quality annotations remain limited
(Section~\ref{sec:related-work}). Producing these labels is
labor-intensive and requires substantial expert time~\cite{Willemink2020Preparing,Zhang2024Advances}.
An efficient workflow should allow fetal cardiologists to
focus on image interpretation rather than manually recording
frame ranges and labels. In the spreadsheet workflow evaluated
here, experts review cine in a separate viewer and type
annotations into spreadsheets. Without checks against file
length and existing labels, overlapping, missing, or
out-of-range entries may go unnoticed.
