To address these challenges, we developed the Cardiologist
Annotation Tool (CAT) in close collaboration with fetal
cardiologists to make efficient use of their limited annotation
time. CAT reads DICOM cine directly, preloads neighboring files,
and supports keyboard-based labeling of cardiac views and
image quality (Figure~\ref{fig:figure1_overview}).
Annotations are stored as change points, ensuring that
each frame receives a single view and quality label while
preventing structural data-entry errors by design.
\placefigure{figure1_overview}
This study presents three contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A dedicated fetal echocardiography annotation tool that
  records cardiac views and image quality frame by frame directly
  on DICOM cine and common video and image formats, while preventing structural data-entry errors
  by design. To our knowledge, no published tool combines these features.
  \item An expert audit of 25 fetal echocardiography studies
  (15 normal and 10 CHD) comparing CAT with spreadsheet
  annotation. Using a predefined error codebook and
  validated detectors, we quantified data-entry errors,
  examined their association with CHD, and assessed
  annotation agreement and granularity.
  \item A randomized crossover study involving six medical
  students to compare annotation throughput and view
  accuracy against an expert reference. Frame-based
  throughput was higher with CAT for all six students,
  without a detectable difference in view accuracy.
\end{enumerate}
\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related-work}
\subsection{Annotation practices in fetal ultrasound}
Published fetal cardiac datasets illustrate different approaches
to expert labeling. Arnaout et al.\ assigned every frame to one
of five screening views or a non-target category and used only
diagnostically adequate views to train their view classifier~\cite{Arnaout2021Ensemble}.
For HeartAssist, one expert
classified 65,324 images from 2,985 fetuses into ten cardiac
views~\cite{Kim2025HeartAssist}. Taks{\o}e-Vester et al.\
used LabelMe to annotate still images of eight cardiac planes
and assign structure-level quality scores~\cite{TaksoeeVester2024Quality},
while Nurmaini et al.\
outlined cardiac structures in frames extracted from ultrasound
videos~\cite{Nurmaini2021Deep}. Other fetal ultrasound datasets
used customized CVAT or in-house interfaces to annotate
images~\cite{Prabakaran2023FPUS23,Pu2026FUSEP,
BurgosArtizzu2020Evaluation}. These studies demonstrate
variation in annotation granularity and how image quality
is assessed.
\subsection{Existing annotation tools}
General-purpose tools such as LabelMe, VIA, CVAT, and Label
Studio support image labeling, video-frame annotation, or
temporal segments~\cite{Russell2008LabelMe,LabelmeGitHub2026,Dutta2019VIA,
CVATDocs2026,LabelStudioDocs2026,Tkachenko2020LabelStudio}. Their flexibility
allows project-specific label schemas, but fetal cardiac
views and image-quality grades require configuration.
Ultrasound-specific tools provide more tailored capabilities.
Annotation Web supports ultrasound-video annotation, including
sequence-level image-quality fields, and has been adapted for
regional quality scoring in adult echocardiography~\cite{Smistad2021AnnotationWeb,VanDeVyver2025Regional}.
SlicerUltrasound processes DICOM ultrasound cine and can attach
classification labels to entire loops~\cite{SlicerUltrasoundGitHub2026}.
Most directly relevant,
an Oxford fetal heart tool labels each video frame with one of
three cardiac views and a visibility state. It works with
screen-captured video rather than native DICOM cine and does
not record a frame-level quality grade~\cite{Bridge2021HeartAnnotation,Bridge2017Thesis}.
Across these tools, support for native DICOM cine, frame-level
fetal cardiac view and image-quality labeling, and prevention
of structural data-entry errors remains distributed across
different platforms. None of the reviewed tools reported an
evaluation against another annotation method. To address these
gaps, CAT was designed to combine these features in a portable
desktop application for macOS and Windows, without requiring
package installation or annotation configuration. We evaluated
CAT against traditional spreadsheet annotation in complementary
expert and student studies.
\subsection{Automated and weakly supervised annotation}
Other approaches reduce the need for manual frame-level
annotation. SonoNet derived standard-plane labels from
freeze frames saved during routine scanning~\cite{Baumgartner2017SonoNet}.
The iFIND and PULSE projects
used freeze events, on-screen text, and machine settings
to recover information from recorded scans~\cite{Venturini2025Whole,Sharma2021Knowledge,
Drukker2021Transforming}. In adult echocardiography,
stored DICOM view tags have also provided labels~\cite{UlloaCerna2021Deep}.
Another study used AI to extract video clips of the standard cardiac views from full fetal
scans for remote second review by experts~\cite{Day2025Video}. These approaches can
accelerate dataset preparation, but stored labels reflect what sonographers chose to save,
and standard-plane detectors trained on normal hearts may miss frames that show structural
defects~\cite{Tan2020Automated}. Expert-reviewed frame-level labels therefore remain
valuable for evaluating automated view and quality predictions.
