\subsection{Development of the annotation tool}
CAT was developed in close collaboration with fetal cardiologists to
maximize the efficiency of their limited annotation time. The software
was implemented in Python with a Tkinter-based graphical user interface
and is distributed as a bundled executable for macOS and Windows that runs
locally on the annotator's computer without installing Python or other packages. The initial version was
developed in one month roughly, followed by two months
of testing and iterative refinement based on feedback from fetal
cardiologists. During this period, additional features were incorporated
to address their annotation needs and improve usability. The finalized
version was used consistently in both experimental studies.
\subsection{Software and annotation workflow}
CAT opens a study folder, validates its files,
and reads DICOM cine files directly, as well
as MPEG-4 (MP4) video and image files (Figure~\ref{fig:figure2_cat_software}). While one file is
displayed, neighboring files are decoded in background threads (preloading). The annotator
plays or scrubs through the cine and labels frames with single keys; changes are saved
automatically, and labels are exported as comma-separated values (CSV) files.
