\subsection{Annotation model}
Each frame receives one of eight view labels: the abdominal (situs) view (ABDO), apical
four-chamber view (A4CH), left ventricular outflow tract (LVOT), right ventricular outflow
tract (RVOT), three-vessel view (3VV), three-vessel and trachea view (3VT), non-target (NT) for
a frame that shows none of these six target views, and ``?'' (uncertain) for frames to
recheck. No frame was labeled ``?'' in either study. The target views correspond to the views
of the fetal cardiac screening examination~\cite{Carvalho2023ISUOG}; the aortic and ductal arch views are not
among them, so such frames were labeled NT. Target-view frames also receive a quality score on
a three-level ordinal scale, judged on the visibility of the cardiac structures that define the
view: 2, clearly visible without occlusion or noise; 1, some noise or not fully clear, but
acceptable; 0, not adequately visible. Quality has no meaning for NT frames and was ignored for
them in every analysis.
The annotator records a label only where it changes, and the label holds until the next change
point in the same file or to the end of the file, so the segments tile each file without gaps
or overlaps. When a file is first displayed, CAT records NT with quality 2 for its first frame.
\subsection{Error prevention by design}
Frame ranges typed into a spreadsheet beside a separate viewer can overlap under different
views, leave frames uncovered, run beyond the end of a file, or be unreadable. CAT prevents
these structural data-entry errors by construction (Table~\ref{tab:table3_error_prevention}):
each frame has one label, ranges derive from change points, frame numbers come from the
display, labels from fixed keys, and file names from disk. 
