\subsection{Studies}
\textbf{Expert audit.} Pediatric and fetal cardiologists annotated 25 fetal
echocardiography studies, selected at random within two groups: 15 normal
studies and 10 with CHD. In the traditional pass, two experts reviewed the original DICOM files in
an image viewer and typed frame ranges into a spreadsheet of their own design, with one row per
file and one column per view and quality level. After an interval of 3 to 5 months,
three experts annotated the same files with CAT where one expert took part in both passes. The four experts had 17.5 years of experience on average. In this setting, annotation time was not
measured and there was no reference standard to adjudicate disagreements between both methods. The analysis set comprised 1,746 files (247,477 frames) present in both sources.
\textbf{Student crossover study.} A total of six medical students each annotated two
normal scans, one with each method, in a randomized two-period crossover:
three students were randomly selected for Group A and the remaining three formed Group B. Group~A (student1--student3) used the traditional spreadsheet first and
Group~B (student4--student6) used CAT first. The students were not told which group they were
in or whether other students annotated the same scans, and each annotated separately from the
others. Beforehand, the students were trained by an expert on one example scan similar to the two study scans. Each scan included invalid decoy files, which the students were instructed to leave unannotated
(Table~\ref{tab:table2_study_design}). Each student received renamed, shuffled video and image
copies of the scans and recorded the start and end of each session in a template spreadsheet; the
spreadsheet template for traditional annotation restricted view and quality to the permitted
values. The students' labels were scored against the expert reference, which was created with CAT and
revised twice by experts. 
\subsection{Outcomes}
\textbf{Data-entry errors.} Before any error was counted, we froze an error codebook
of 21 codes in six families: formatting, unreadable or
ambiguous entries, range validity, overlaps, coverage gaps and row identity. Each code has a
certainty tier, provable (wrong by the spreadsheet's own logic or by the file length) or requiring
the images, and a severity graded by its effect on a
training dataset (critical: a wrong or contradictory view label, or labels on the wrong file;
major: labels lost or quality off by two levels; Table~\ref{tab:table3_error_prevention}).
The main measure was the share of files with a
provable finding of major or critical severity.
\textbf{Agreement between methods.} On frames with exactly one traditional view, we computed
view agreement, Cohen's $\kappa$ and Gwet's first-order agreement coefficient (AC1)~\cite{Cohen1960Coefficient,Gwet2008Computing}, and
on frames with the same target view in both methods, linearly weighted $\kappa$ for quality~\cite{Cohen1968Weighted}.
Boundary agreement was the share of view-change boundaries of one method that had a boundary
of the other method within $\pm k$ frames, for the range of tolerances $k$ shown in Figure~\ref{fig:figure4_expert_agreement}c. Each traditional frame was classified as
agreeing with CAT, differing from CAT, unlabeled, or conflicting. Because annotators and method
changed together, this represents a method-plus-rater agreement.
\textbf{Student measures.} Outcomes included self-reported annotation
time, reference view segments per minute, labeled and correctly labeled
frames per minute, view accuracy, per-view recall, and data-entry errors
identified using an adapted codebook. Reference segments per minute
was calculated using the fixed number of expert-defined segments,
independent of how students divided the clips. Correctly labeled frames
were those matching the expert reference. View accuracy was the
proportion of reference frames correctly labeled, with unlabeled or
conflicting frames counted as incorrect. Predefined flags identified
unreliable time records, protocol deviations (including annotation of
at least half the decoys), and outliers (robust $|z| > 3.5$).
Flagged observations were retained in the primary analysis.
\subsection{Statistical analysis}
In the expert audit, per-study error rates were assigned Wilson
95\
Pooled error rates and agreement measures were assigned CIs using
2,000 study-level bootstrap resamples stratified by CHD
status~\cite{Efron1993Introduction}. Error rates in normal and CHD
studies were compared using a permutation test (100,000 permutations).
A logistic generalized estimating equation (GEE)
model~\cite{Liang1986Longitudinal} assessed associations between
major or critical data-entry errors and CHD status, log frame count,
file type, and file position, accounting for clustering within studies.
In the student study, paired CAT-to-spreadsheet ratios were calculated
for annotation time and throughput measures, while paired differences
were used for view accuracy. Ratios were analyzed on the log scale.
We used exact two-sided paired permutation tests~\cite{Ernst2004Permutation}
and Hodges--Lehmann estimates with exact CIs (96.9\
pairs)~\cite{Hodges1963Estimates,Hollander2013Nonparametric}.
\textit{P} values were not adjusted for multiplicity.
A separate randomized crossover analysis assessed the method effect
while accounting for the combined influence of scan and annotation
period. Sensitivity analyses included exclusion of the student with
unreliable timing, and CAT active time derived from its event logs. All analyses were conducted in Python.
