\begin{abstract}
Accurate segmentation of the mitral valve from three-dimensional transesophageal echocardiography (3D TEE) is severely restricted by the scarcity of publicly available expert-annotated volumetric datasets. To address this critical limitation, we investigated Fourier Domain Adaptation (FDA), utilizing general-purpose generative-AI images (from platforms like ChatGPT, Google Gemini, and Microsoft MAI) as annotation-free style donors for 3D mitral valve segmentation.
We employed a DeepMitral residual U-Net model on the public MVSeg2023 dataset, comparing six conditions over three runs: real-only training, traditional augmentation, FDA with each generative source, and a non-AI FDA control using real-image style donors, plus an optimization-matched real-only control and a $\beta$ sensitivity analysis. Case-level paired testing showed that FDA with these generative sources led to significant reductions in boundary- and surface-related errors across all three donor conditions relative to the primary real-only baseline.
However, the impact on the core segmentation metric, Generalized Dice Score (GDS), proved generator-dependent. ChatGPT-FDA showed a significant GDS advantage over the optimization-matched control but not over the primary baseline, and Gemini-FDA showed no significant GDS difference. Notably, only MAI-FDA provided a statistically robust and significant increase in GDS compared to the primary baseline. The $\beta$ sensitivity was not robust across training runs.
These findings confirm that FDA remains an important augmentation mechanism. While generative-AI images offer a practical means of acquiring style diversity without new annotations, the non-AI control performed comparably to ChatGPT-FDA, so we do not conclude that this approach is superior to training with real-image donors.
\end{abstract}
\textbf{Index Terms}---mitral valve segmentation, transesophageal echocardiography, synthetic data augmentation, generative AI, Fourier domain adaptation, 3D U-Net, MONAI.
\section{Introduction}
Mitral valve disease, including degenerative mitral regurgitation, functional regurgitation, and rheumatic stenosis, is among the most common structural cardiac conditions requiring surgical or transcatheter intervention~\cite{benjamin2018heart,patwa2025heart}. Three-dimensional transesophageal echocardiography (3D TEE) is the clinical reference modality for assessing mitral valve morphology because it captures the dynamic, saddle-shaped leaflet geometry that two-dimensional imaging cannot fully resolve~\cite{hahn2013guidelines}. Extracting a case-specific mitral valve surface model from a 3D TEE volume is essential for pre-operative planning, in-silico repair simulation, and quantitative assessment of leaflet coaptation, but manual segmentation of a single 3D TEE volume can take a trained operator upwards of two hours~\cite{carnahan2021deepmitral}, making it impractical for routine clinical workflows.
Deep learning has substantially reduced this burden. Carnahan et al.\ introduced DeepMitral, a fully automatic 3D residual U-Net that segments the mitral leaflets directly from raw TEE volumes in seconds rather than hours~\cite{carnahan2021deepmitral}, and the same group subsequently released the associated data through the MVSeg2023 public challenge~\cite{mvseg2023}. Despite this progress, the fundamental data bottleneck of medical image segmentation persists: MVSeg2023 provides only 105 annotated training volumes, an order of magnitude smaller than the datasets that power state-of-the-art segmentation frameworks such as nnU-Net in other anatomical domains~\cite{isensee2021nnunet}. Because 3D TEE annotation requires simultaneous expertise in cardiac anatomy and ultrasound interpretation, this scarcity is unlikely to be resolved through additional manual labelling alone.
A parallel line of work has explored synthetic data generation as a remedy for small medical imaging datasets. Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) have been used to augment liver lesion classification~\cite{fridadar2018gan}, and surveys of GAN-based medical image synthesis document dozens of similar applications across radiology and pathology~\cite{kazeminia2020gans, yicomputingsurveys}. More recently, large-scale vision-language foundation models have been shown capable of synthesizing clinically coherent chest X-rays conditioned on radiology reports~\cite{bluethgen2025vision}, and a concurrent study demonstrated that a general-purpose commercial image generator (Nano Banana, the image-generation component of Google Gemini) can produce synthetic chest radiographs realistic enough to train a pneumonia classifier that generalizes to real-world X-ray datasets with an AUROC of up to $0.923$~\cite{peng2025pneumonia}. 
This study follows Peng et al.~\cite{peng2025pneumonia}, who showed that off-the-shelf, non-medical generative AI tools (requiring no fine-tuning or specialized medical training data) can produce synthetic images with measurable downstream classification utility. We ask whether this paradigm extends from 2D image \emph{classification} to 3D TEE \emph{segmentation}, a substantially harder problem for two reasons. First, segmentation requires a dense, voxel-accurate label for every training example, whereas classification requires a single scalar label, and general-purpose text-to-image generators such as ChatGPT, Gemini, and MAI-Image-2.6 cannot produce such masks. Second, 3D TEE is volumetric while consumer generative models output only 2D raster images, a dimensional mismatch that na\"ive augmentation cannot resolve.
We address both obstacles with a single procedure. Rather than treating synthetic images as independent training examples requiring annotation, we use each 2D image purely as a \emph{style} reference and apply Fourier Domain Adaptation (FDA)~\cite{yang2020fda} to transfer selected low-frequency amplitude components onto a real, already-annotated 3D TEE volume. Because FDA retains the real-image phase, the volume's spatial organization is expected to remain largely unchanged, so we reuse the original expert mask rather than claiming that every anatomical feature is exactly preserved. This turns the 2D-image-to-3D-mask annotation problem into a label-preserving appearance-transfer problem, yielding additional labelled 3D training volumes without new manual segmentation.
We construct three generator-guided FDA training sets, derived from ChatGPT (GPT-Image), Google Gemini (Nano Banana), and Microsoft MAI-Image-2.6, and compare them with a non-AI FDA control, a real-only baseline, and traditional augmentation, giving six experimental conditions. Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
\item We evaluate general-purpose, non-medical generative-AI images as appearance-style donors for 3D TEE mitral valve segmentation.
\item We use slice-wise 2D FDA to bridge the dimensional mismatch between 2D generated images and 3D segmentation training: the synthetic image contributes low-frequency appearance, while the real-image phase and original expert mask are retained, avoiding voxel-level label generation.
\item We perform a controlled three-generator evaluation with a matched-update real-only baseline, a non-AI FDA style-donor control, a provenance-tracked FDA parameter sensitivity analysis, and case-level paired statistical testing across three runs.
\end{enumerate}
\section{Related Work}
\textit{Mitral Valve and Cardiac Structure Segmentation:}
Automated mitral valve segmentation from 3D TEE has progressed from atlas-based and active-contour methods to deep learning. Andreassen et al.\ used a deep convolutional network for mitral annulus segmentation in 3D TEE~\cite{andreassen2020annulus}. Carnahan et al.'s DeepMitral applied a 3D residual U-Net trained with the MONAI framework to segment the full leaflet apparatus directly from raw TEE volumes, achieving strong agreement with manual expert segmentations while reducing processing time from hours to seconds~\cite{carnahan2021deepmitral}. Subsequent work has introduced anatomical constraints into mitral annulus segmentation~\cite{anatomicalconstraints2024} and explored graph-neural-network representations of continuous leaflet motion across the cardiac cycle~\cite{mvgnn2022}. Chen et al.\ proposed an automatic 3D mitral valve leaflet segmentation pipeline validated against quantitative clinical measurements~\cite{chen2023mitralleaflet}. Across this literature, dataset size remains a recurring limitation, with reported training sets ranging from 44 to about 100 annotated volumes~\cite{carnahan2021deepmitral, regurgitantmv2024}---a scale far below that available for more common segmentation targets such as abdominal organs or brain tumors.
\textit{Synthetic Medical Image Generation and GAN-Based Augmentation:}
Frid-Adar et al.\ utilized GAN-generated synthetic CT liver lesions to improve CNN classification performance on a real dataset~\cite{fridadar2018gan}. Kazeminia et al.\ and Yi et al.\ subsequently surveyed the rapid expansion of GAN applications across medical image synthesis, segmentation, and cross-modality translation~\cite{kazeminia2020gans, yicomputingsurveys}. More recently, Bluethgen et al.\ demonstrated that a vision-language foundation model conditioned on radiology reports could synthesize chest radiographs with clinically coherent pathology~\cite{bluethgen2025vision}, illustrating the shift from adversarial architectures toward large pretrained generative foundation models for medical image synthesis.
\textit{General-purpose Generative AI for Medical Data Augmentation:}
Very recently, Peng et al.\ used Nano Banana (Google's general-purpose, non-medical-specialized image generation model) to synthesize 300 chest radiographs and fine-tuned a ResNet-50 classifier exclusively on this synthetic data, achieving an AUROC of $0.923$ on the real-world RSNA Pneumonia Detection dataset and $0.824$ on an independent Chest X-Ray dataset~\cite{peng2025pneumonia}. That study also compared against RoentGen-v2, a domain-specialized diffusion model for CXR generation~\cite{moroianu2025roentgen}, finding that the general-purpose Nano Banana model outperformed the CXR-specialized generator once post-processing (watermark and field-of-view cropping) was applied. 
\textit{Domain Adaptation and Fourier-Based Style Transfer:}
Unsupervised domain adaptation seeks to transfer a model trained on a source domain (e.g., synthetic images) to a target domain (e.g., real images) without target-domain labels, historically through adversarial feature alignment~\cite{hoffman2018cycada}.
Yang and Soatto proposed Fourier Domain Adaptation, showing that
swapping selected low-frequency amplitude components between source
and target images can alter appearance-related statistics while largely
retaining spatial organization~\cite{yang2020fda}. FDA and related
Fourier-based augmentation strategies have since been applied to
source-free medical image domain adaptation~\cite{yang2022fouriermining}.
\section{Dataset and Baseline Model}
\textit{MVSeg2023 Dataset:}
We use the publicly released MVSeg2023 dataset~\cite{mvseg2023}, comprising 3D TEE volumes with voxel-level mitral valve leaflet annotations, partitioned into 105 training, 30 validation, and 40 test volumes. The dataset was originally released with separate anterior- and posterior-leaflet label classes. Following the pretrained DeepMitral model's convention, we merge these into a single binary leaflet class (background vs.\ leaflet) for all experiments, so that results are directly comparable to the publicly released pretrained checkpoint. Voxel spacing varies by volume (approximately $0.2$--$0.7$\,mm per axis), reflecting the heterogeneous acquisition protocols typical of clinical 3D TEE. The 40 held-out test volumes were used exclusively for evaluation and were not used to construct any FDA-adapted training volumes.
\textit{DeepMitral Model:}
We use the official DeepMitral implementation~\cite{carnahan2021deepmitral}, a 3D residual U-Net built on MONAI~\cite{cardoso2022monai} with five resolution levels (channel widths 16--32--64--128--256), stride-2 downsampling at each level, two residual units per level, batch normalization, and no dropout. 
Training uses random patch-based cropping (with a patch size of $96^3$ voxels, with foreground-biased sampling). 
\section{Methodology}
\subsection{Overview}
Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline} summarizes the pipeline. For each of the three generative-AI tools, we (i) generate 40 synthetic 2D TEE images with structured prompts; (ii) apply slice-wise Fourier Domain Adaptation to transfer low-frequency appearance from each 2D donor onto a selected real training volume, retaining the real-image phase and reusing the original expert mask unchanged; (iii) combine the 40 adapted volumes with the 105 real training volumes to form a 145-volume training set; and (iv) train DeepMitral with an identical architecture and optimization protocol. The validation and test splits consist exclusively of real, expert-annotated volumes.
Six conditions are compared: a real-only baseline, traditional augmentation, and four FDA conditions (ChatGPT-, Gemini-, MAI-, and non-AI FDA). Because the FDA sets contain more volumes than the baseline and therefore receive more optimization updates, we additionally trained a 540-update real-only control matching their budget. A $\beta$-sensitivity analysis is also performed. 
