\subsection{Synthetic Image Generation}
For each of three generators, ChatGPT (GPT-Image), Google Gemini (Nano Banana), and Microsoft MAI-Image-2.6, we generated 40 synthetic 2D images of the mid-esophageal TEE mitral valve view. Following the structured-prompt approach of Peng et al.~\cite{peng2025pneumonia}, an initial prompt requested a plain grayscale ultrasound sector image without on-image text, ECG traces, or angle indicators (which early generations contained), followed by class-specific prompts for normal morphology and for variants consistent with mitral regurgitation, stenosis, and prolapse, to promote structural diversity. All outputs were manually inspected, and images with encoding artifacts were discarded before augmentation.
\subsection{Slice-wise 2D Fourier Domain Adaptation}
Given a real 3D TEE volume $I_r$ with expert leaflet mask $M_r$ and a synthetic 2D image $I_s$, we applied conventional 2D FDA~\cite{yang2020fda} independently to each slice of $I_r$ (i.e., FDA was not implemented as a 3D Fourier transform). Each donor was paired with one real training volume and used for all of its slices. For each slice, the donor was resized to the slice dimensions, both images were normalized to $[0,1]$, and a 2D FFT was computed. The central low-frequency region of the slice's amplitude spectrum was replaced with the donor's corresponding components using $\beta=0.01$, while the slice's phase spectrum was retained. An inverse FFT reconstructed each adapted slice, and the slices were restacked in their original order to form the adapted volume $I_r^{\prime}$. Because the real phase is retained, spatial organization is expected to remain largely intact, so $M_r$ was reused unchanged: the donor contributes low-frequency appearance variation while supervision is inherited from the real volume. This lets generated 2D images add appearance diversity to 3D training without synthetic 3D masks or additional annotation.
For each generator, the 40 donors were paired with 40 distinct real training volumes, selected from the 105 with a fixed random seed of 42, which is independent of the training seeds (1, 2, and 3). The same selection and $\beta=0.01$ were used for all generators (i.e., $\beta$ was not tuned per generator).
\subsection{Training Configuration}
In order to realize a standardized, rigorously validated protocol, we held the network architecture, optimizer, patch size, and epoch-based training schedule fixed across all six experimental conditions, so that performance differences reflect the training data rather than the protocol. 
All experiments used the DeepMitral 3D residual U-Net with $96^3$-voxel patches, batch size 4, \texttt{drop\_last=True}, NovoGrad with learning rate $1\times10^{-3}$, the same Dice--cross-entropy loss, and a 15-epoch schedule, with three independent training runs per condition.
The primary real-only baseline (105 volumes) yields 26 mini-batches per epoch, i.e., 390 updates; each FDA condition (145 volumes) yields 36 mini-batches per epoch, i.e., 540 updates. The primary baseline used the 105 real training volumes without FDA or other synthetic augmentation, and its three runs were taken from archived original runs. The 540-update real-only control used the same 105 volumes, network, loss, optimizer, batch size, and three-run protocol. The validation and test volumes were never added to the training set.
The traditional-augmentation comparator used the 105 real volumes with online MONAI augmentations: \texttt{RandAffined} (probability $0.5$; rotations up to $\pm5^\circ$ per axis; scaling up to $\pm10\
\subsection{Standardized Evaluation Metrics}
Segmentation quality is assessed with the Generalized Dice Score (GDS) for overlap, the 95th-percentile Hausdorff Distance (HD95) for boundary discrepancy, and Surface Distance for mean surface error. Since the anterior and posterior leaflets are merged into one foreground class, GDS reduces to the standard Dice coefficient; we retain the term GDS to reflect the MONAI metric used. All test metrics were computed with an identical protocol: predictions were defined on the DeepMitral 0.3-mm isotropic evaluation grid, the ground truth was resampled to the prediction shape and affine with nearest-neighbor interpolation, and all three metrics were computed on the binary foreground with background excluded. Distances were converted from voxels to mm using the isotropic spacing.
\section{Results}
\subsection{Statistical Analysis Methods}
\label{sec:stats}
This subsection describes how the reported numbers and $p$-values were obtained, for the ease of the reader.
\textit{Case-level aggregation.} Model performance is reported as mean $\pm$ sample standard deviation across three independent training runs. The public MVSeg2023 release provides anonymized, single-frame end-diastolic volumes whose metadata are stripped except for image size and spacing; the dataset description states that each case represents one patient, but no patient identifiers or clinical attributes are released. The unit of analysis is therefore the test case (volume), and independence across cases is assumed from the dataset description rather than verified. Because all runs are evaluated on the same 40 held-out cases, conditions are compared at the case level: each metric was first averaged across the three runs for every case and condition, giving one value per case and condition ($n=40$) that uses all runs rather than a single seed.
\textit{Paired comparisons.} For two conditions, the paired difference is computed for each case and defined so that positive values indicate improvement (GDS increase; HD95 and Surface Distance reduction). Significance is assessed with a two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test, a non-parametric test of whether the paired differences are centered on zero that does not assume normally distributed differences ($W$ denotes its test statistic). Uncertainty in the mean paired difference is given by a paired bootstrap 95\
\textit{Multiple comparisons.} Because three metrics are tested per comparison, Holm correction was applied across the three metrics within each pre-specified comparison, and all $p$-values reported below are Holm-adjusted ($p_{\mathrm{Holm}}$) unless stated otherwise. As a conservative robustness check, Holm correction was also applied jointly across all nine generator-by-metric comparisons against the 390-update baseline ($p_{\mathrm{Holm,9}}$). The comparisons performed were: ChatGPT-, Gemini-, and MAI-FDA against the 390-update real-only baseline; ChatGPT-FDA and the non-AI FDA control against the 540-update real-only control; and the non-AI FDA control against ChatGPT-FDA.
\textit{FDA Parameter sensitivity.} For the FDA parameter $\beta$ sensitivity experiment, case-level metrics were averaged over runs for each $\beta$ and compared with an overall Friedman test, a non-parametric test for differences across three or more related conditions, with Holm-corrected pairwise Wilcoxon tests and paired bootstrap intervals for follow-up. The Friedman analysis was repeated after excluding Seed~1 (averaging Seeds~2 and~3) to assess sensitivity to between-run variability. Friedman $p$-values are reported as obtained.
\subsection{Quantitative Segmentation Performance}
\label{sec:quant}
\begin{table*}[t]
