\section{Discussion}
The standardized three-run analysis shows that FDA-based augmentation does not produce a uniform improvement across every segmentation metric. Among the six experimental conditions reported in Table~\ref{tab:results}, MAI-FDA had the numerically highest mean GDS, the non-AI FDA control had the numerically lowest mean HD95, and Gemini-FDA had the numerically lowest mean Surface Distance. All four FDA-based conditions substantially reduced mean boundary-error metrics relative to the primary real-only baseline, whereas traditional augmentation did not improve the primary baseline. However, since the parameters of traditional augmentation were fixed a priori and not tuned, one should not conclude that the conventional augmentation was ineffective.
The control experiments further refine the interpretation of these results. 
Relative to the 390-update baseline, the GDS effect was donor-dependent: only MAI-FDA gave a significant paired improvement ($\Delta=0.0111$, $p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=0.00123$), whereas HD95 and Surface Distance were significantly reduced for all three generator-guided conditions. The most reproducible effect is thus reduced boundary and surface error, while improved overlap is not shared across generators.
The 540-update real-only control addresses optimization budget. ChatGPT-FDA showed a significant GDS advantage over it, so its result is not attributable solely to extra updates. Because this control had lower GDS but better boundary metrics than the 390-update baseline, longer training appears to shift the overlap--boundary trade-off. The two controls are therefore treated as complementary. 
Real training-image style donors yielded GDS and HD95 similar to ChatGPT-FDA (no significant difference), so FDA is an important mechanism but generative-AI donor content is not shown to be uniquely beneficial. Its practical value is as an annotation-free source of 2D appearance variation transferable to labelled real volumes, rather than a generator of valid 3D volumes or labels. 
In other words, generative-AI donor may be particularly useful when additional real appearance donors are unavailable or costly to collect.
Finally, The FDA parameter $\beta$ sensitivity analysis therefore points to substantial between-run variability and does not support a universal claim that one tested $\beta$ value is optimal.
\textbf{Limitations.} First, MVSeg2023 is anonymized, with no patient identifiers or diagnostic metadata, so independence across cases is assumed and performance could not be stratified by valve pathology. Also, the test cohort has only 40 real MVSeg2023 volumes. Thus, external validation on an independent 3D TEE dataset is needed. Second, FDA alters appearance statistics but creates no new valve anatomy or pathology. Third, only three runs per condition limit inference on between-run variability. Fourth, clinical morphometrics (e.g., mitral annular area, inter-commissural distance, tenting area) could not be derived because the binary masks lack the required landmarks and reference planes. Fifth, the six-condition comparison reported in Table~\ref{tab:results} used an epoch-matched design (390 updates for the primary real-only baseline, 540 for FDA conditions). 
We therefore report both a direct paired comparison with the original 390-update Table~1 baseline and a separate 540-update real-only control that matches the FDA optimization budget. The GDS conclusions differ between these comparisons: the ChatGPT-FDA GDS increase was not significant relative to the original 390-update baseline but was significant relative to the 540-update matched control. Accordingly, overlap and boundary metrics are interpreted separately rather than treating either baseline comparison as universally definitive.
\section{Conclusion}
We evaluated general-purpose generative-AI images as 2D appearance donors for label-preserving, slice-wise Fourier Domain Adaptation in 3D mitral-valve segmentation. In the standardized six-condition three-run comparison, MAI-FDA achieved the numerically highest mean GDS (0.8309 $\pm$ 0.0129), the non-AI FDA control the lowest mean HD95 (3.0208 $\pm$ 0.3519 mm), and Gemini-FDA the lowest mean Surface Distance (0.5712 $\pm$ 0.1460 mm). Relative to the 390-update real-only baseline, HD95 and Surface Distance were significantly reduced for all three generative-AI donors, making improved boundary accuracy the most consistent finding, whereas the GDS effect was generator-dependent (significant only for MAI-FDA: $\Delta=0.0111$, $p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=0.00123$), although ChatGPT-FDA also showed a significant GDS advantage over the 540-update matched control. Since a non-AI FDA control using real training images as style donors performed comparably on GDS and HD95, this supports FDA as an important augmentation mechanism without establishing that generative-AI donors are superior to real-image donors. Generative models nonetheless offer a practical, annotation-free source of 2D appearance diversity that can be transferred to already-labelled real 3D volumes without requiring synthetic voxel-level masks.
\section*{Appendix}
A common structured prompt template was used to generate synthetic 2D TEE images with ChatGPT (GPT-Image), Google Gemini (Nano Banana), and Microsoft MAI-Image-2.6:
{\small
\noindent
\textit{
Generate 10 realistic synthetic transesophageal echocardiography (TEE)
grayscale B-mode ultrasound images of the mitral valve, representing
10 different cases, generated individually as separate images.
Use a true mid-esophageal TEE mitral-valve view rather than a
transthoracic apical four-chamber view. The left atrium should appear
in the near field, the mitral valve should be prominently centered,
and the left ventricle should appear deeper in the ultrasound sector.
Focus mainly on the left atrium, mitral valve, and proximal left
ventricle. Clearly depict two separate mitral leaflets: the anterior mitral
leaflet (AML) and posterior mitral leaflet (PML), with the AML generally
longer than the PML. Both leaflets should arise from separate annular
attachment points, with visible leaflet bodies, free edges, and tips. Use a realistic fan-shaped grayscale B-mode ultrasound sector on a
black background with natural speckle noise and realistic echogenicity.
Introduce variation among cases in age, sex, cardiac phase, probe
angle, sector depth, image gain, speckle pattern, mild acoustic
shadowing, and heart size. Do not include any text, anatomical labels, letters, numbers, ECG
traces, color Doppler, depth markers, angle indicators, machine
interface elements, logos, or watermarks. Do not depict the mitral
valve as a single continuous bright horizontal line.
}
}
For normal mitral-valve images, the prompt additionally specified thin,
pliable, normally mobile leaflets with complete coaptation and no
thickening, calcification, stenosis, or prolapse. For mitral
regurgitation, the prompt requested incomplete leaflet coaptation with
a visible gap, redundant or thickened leaflet tissue, and/or a
prolapsing leaflet segment without color Doppler. For mitral stenosis,
the prompt requested thickened and/or calcified leaflets, restricted
diastolic opening, reduced leaflet separation, leaflet doming, and a
narrowed mitral orifice. For mitral valve prolapse, the prompt requested
one or both leaflets billowing or prolapsing toward the left atrium
during systole. The same core anatomical and imaging constraints were used with
ChatGPT, Gemini, and MAI-Image-2.6. Minor wording adjustments were made
during iterative generation when necessary to remove unwanted text,
interface elements, incorrect viewing geometry, or other non-diagnostic
artifacts.
